-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- ABC-27 + ABC-28 — the rebook ROUND becomes a typed entity with durable command receipts, and
--                   the member-open notification becomes durable outbox state instead of a
--                   per-cycle email loop.
--
-- Timestamp `20261118120000` is coordinator-issued exclusively to this unit inside the reserved
-- pre-U2 ABC block (`20261118110000`–`20261118190000`), after a fresh origin refresh and an
-- active-worktree/all-ref/history scan. It sorts after the ABC-16/17 containment migration and
-- before U2's `20261119…` block. It is NOT self-allocated.
--
-- WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE MIGRATION. ABC-16/ABC-17 containment (20261118110000) withdrew
-- authority from caller-authored evidence. It did not give the rebook round an identity. This
-- one does, additively, after it — burying a new entity inside a containment migration would
-- make both un-reviewable and un-revertable as separate decisions.
--
-- WHAT WAS WRONG (the model, not one defect):
--
--   * A "round" existed only as `cycles.settings.rebook_round_id` — a JSON string minted by an
--     Edge function. Nothing typed it, nothing constrained it to one academy, nothing stopped a
--     manager from writing any value into it through the broad cycle-update policy, and the
--     notifier discovered siblings with `settings->>'rebook_round_id' = <string>`, which is a
--     text comparison against caller-writable JSON.
--   * The notifier was a per-cycle direct sender. It claimed ONE cycle, truncated at the first
--     20 candidates, emailed from inside the request, and recorded success by appending recipient
--     keys into that same JSON blob. Every one of those is a place where a crash, a rate limit or
--     a 21st cycle silently loses recipients — and the JSON marker cannot distinguish "sent",
--     "skipped because they had no address" and "never considered".
--   * Eligibility was computed once, per cycle, and then frozen into an email. A recipient who
--     lost their seat, whose window closed, or whose sibling filled up between detection and send
--     still got the invitation.
--
-- ABC-28 CORRECTED THREE THINGS IN THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS FILE, and they are worth stating
-- because each was a real hole rather than a style preference:
--
--   1. A command identity on the ROUND row covers creation only. Extension had no command
--      identity and no receipt at all, so a retried extension could not be told from a second
--      extension. Command identity now lives in its own normalized table, `rebook_round_commands`,
--      with an immutable expected CHILD SET as rows — one receipt per create AND per extend.
--   2. Separate `attach` and `open` calls let one request commit HALF a sibling set. A
--      materializer running between two of those calls would freeze a partial round and then
--      permanently exclude the rest. Creation and extension are now ONE atomic, round-locked
--      batch command. There is no piecemeal attach/open surface left to race against.
--   3. The relation guard covered INSERT and UPDATE but not DELETE, so an academy manager could
--      delete an attached cycle straight out of a frozen sibling set. DELETE now fails closed,
--      and every reference in this unit is `ON DELETE RESTRICT` — notification and audit evidence
--      is never silently cascaded away. Removal is a future round-locked lifecycle command
--      composed with U2 erasure/retention, and is deliberately NOT invented here.
--
-- WHAT THIS INSTALLS:
--
--   §0  A loud preflight. Nothing in this file adopts a pre-existing object by name.
--   §1  `public.rebook_rounds` — UUID entity identity, tenant column, canonical label + window
--       boundaries, an explicit lifecycle, the immutable cursor-free recipient snapshot used by
--       continuation's anti-join, and an extension-closed marker stamped when materialization
--       BEGINS (that is when the sibling set freezes).
--   §2  `public.rebook_round_commands` + `public.rebook_round_command_children` — durable UUID
--       command receipts with a server-derived SHA-256 fingerprint and a normalized, indexed,
--       immutable expected child set.
--   §3  `cycles.rebook_round_id` — a typed, immutable, tenant-coherent relation with a composite
--       FK, structural column privileges, invariant triggers, and DELETE refusal.
--   §4  ONE atomic round-locked create/extend batch command. No piecemeal attach/open, no
--       cross-request lease, no Edge-owned partial cleanup.
--   §5  Write-once per-cycle member-open materialization evidence.
--   §6  The canonical `rebook_member_open_player` event, a typed outbox round reference, and an
--       event-specific semantic uniqueness index.
--   §7  Terminal outcomes: a generic, default-OFF `record_terminal_outcomes` event flag.
--   §8  A bounded, resumable, service-only materializer.
--   §9  Send-time re-checks layered into the ONE live gate the workers already call.
--   §10 Retirement of the old per-cycle claim/checkpoint RPCs, the backfill, and install
--       assertions that prove this migration's own postconditions.
--
-- WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not send anything, does not delete a JSON mirror, does not
-- schedule or unschedule cron, and does not touch a single existing row's audience. The JSON
-- keys stay as compatibility MIRRORS with a documented removal condition (§10).
-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

-- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- §0. Preflight — refuse to adopt anything.
-- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- ONE PINNED NAME-RESOLUTION ENVIRONMENT, ESTABLISHED BEFORE ANY CODE OF THIS UNIT IS COMPILED
-- (Stage 7.4-A review rounds 2–4). Every deparse this unit compares — policy expressions,
-- constraint definitions, trigger definitions — suppresses schema qualifiers for path-visible
-- relations, so a hardened caller path would falsely report drift and a leading foreign schema
-- could falsely pass one; `pg_temp` sits implicitly FIRST for relation lookup unless explicitly
-- placed, so a caller-planted temporary twin of a catalog name (`pg_rewrite`, `pg_class`, …)
-- could satisfy a zero-expectation gate with empty rows; and a `DO` block's DECLARE section is
-- compiled BEFORE its first statement runs, so a pin issued inside a block cannot protect that
-- block's own type resolution from a caller-path domain whose CHECK runs hostile code and
-- re-sets the path. This statement closes all three at once: it is a grammar-level utility
-- command — no expression, type or function resolution happens while it executes, so there is
-- nothing for a caller path to intercept — it runs before the first DO block exists, and it is
-- transaction-local, so nothing survives the install. `pg_catalog` leads (every catalog name is
-- catalog-resolved), `public` follows (deparses render public objects unqualified, matching the
-- pinned baselines), `pg_temp` is explicitly LAST. §10c's temp manifest is written with an
-- explicit `pg_temp.` prefix and is unaffected.
SET LOCAL search_path = pg_catalog, public, pg_temp;

-- ── THE EARLIEST ENVIRONMENT GUARD, AND WHAT IT DOES AND DOES NOT PROVE ─────────────────────
--
-- WHERE WHOLE-FILE ATOMICITY ACTUALLY COMES FROM. It comes from the owner-approved external
-- executor contract (`APPROVE_D7_ATOMIC_MIGRATION_AUTHORITY_OPTION_A_ENVELOPE_AND_REVIEW_LOOP_
-- AMENDMENT_V1`): the declared deploying role applies THE COMPLETE FILE as one explicit
-- `READ COMMITTED` transaction, with no statement chunking, no connection switch and no
-- intermediate commit. That contract — verified by the deployment runbook, not by anything here —
-- is the SOLE proof that this file ran whole. Nothing in this file can prove it, and nothing in
-- this file claims to.
--
-- WHY NOT: A MIGRATION-LOCAL MARKER CANNOT SEE ITS OWN FUTURE. An earlier revision wrote this
-- transaction's id into a transaction-local setting and re-read it one statement later. That
-- proves exactly one thing — those two ADJACENT statements shared a transaction — and it was
-- presented as if it proved the file was not split. It does not: a runner that groups the file
-- into a few large chunks satisfies it, and NOTHING observed before a statement can constrain what
-- a runner does after it. The marker is deleted rather than weakened, because a check that reads
-- as a guarantee it cannot give is worse than no check at all.
--
-- WHAT THIS GUARD DOES PROVE, WHICH IS A PROPERTY OF THE PRESENT MOMENT ONLY. It refuses, before
-- this file resolves a single unqualified name or writes a single row, when the CURRENT connection
-- is not one this migration may run on:
--
--   * the search path in force is not the one the statement above pinned — so either that pin is
--     not in effect on this connection, or some other path is;
--   * the transaction is not `READ COMMITTED` — every serialization claim in this file rests on a
--     fresh per-statement snapshot;
--   * the transaction is `READ ONLY` — this migration writes, and would otherwise fail later, in
--     the middle, on an unrelated statement.
--
-- WHY THE PATH CHECK IS FIRST, AND WHY THIS BLOCK IS WRITTEN THE WAY IT IS. `pg_temp` is
-- implicitly FIRST for relation lookup, and a leading schema in an ambient path shadows types and
-- functions for everything compiled afterwards. Measured on this server version: with an ambient
-- `search_path` of `<foreign>, pg_catalog, public`, a caller-created DOMAIN named `text` is what
-- the very next block's `v_encoding text := …` declaration resolves to, its `CHECK` runs the
-- caller's function, and on a runner that commits per statement that write STAYS. So this guard
-- must both come before that block and be immune to the same trick itself: every type is written
-- `pg_catalog.text`, every function call is `pg_catalog.`-qualified, and every comparison is
-- spelled `OPERATOR(pg_catalog.<>)` so no schema in the path can supply the operator. It reads
-- three settings and compares three strings; there is nothing else in it to intercept.
--
-- IT TAKES NO LOCK, WRITES NOTHING, AND NEEDS NO PRIVILEGE, so it cannot itself become a
-- deployment condition. A refusal here happens before `CREATE ROLE`, before any `GRANT`, and
-- before any catalog read that could be redirected — so a refusal really does leave the database
-- as it found it, and says so.
DO $abc27_envelope$
DECLARE
  v_path pg_catalog.text;
  v_iso  pg_catalog.text;
  v_ro   pg_catalog.text;
BEGIN
  v_path := pg_catalog.current_setting('search_path');
  v_iso  := pg_catalog.current_setting('transaction_isolation');
  v_ro   := pg_catalog.current_setting('transaction_read_only');

  -- (1) THE PATH THIS FILE PINNED IS THE PATH IN FORCE. Checked first, because until it holds
  --     every later unqualified name in this file is resolved by somebody else's path.
  IF v_path OPERATOR(pg_catalog.<>) 'pg_catalog, public, pg_temp' THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 envelope: this connection resolves names as "%", not as the "pg_catalog, public, pg_temp" this file pins — refusing before resolving anything under it', v_path
      USING ERRCODE = '55000',
            HINT = 'This file opens with SET LOCAL search_path = pg_catalog, public, pg_temp, and every unqualified name in it is written against exactly that path. Seeing a different one means the pin is not in effect on this connection — a session or role path is overriding it, or the statement that set it did not run in this transaction. This is a statement about the CONNECTION AS IT IS NOW; it says nothing about how the rest of this file will be executed, which only the declared deploying role can guarantee. Nothing has been written.';
  END IF;

  -- (2) READ COMMITTED, on every path. The lock-then-recheck argument the whole install rests on
  --     is a property of READ COMMITTED alone.
  IF v_iso OPERATOR(pg_catalog.<>) 'read committed' THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 envelope: this transaction is running at isolation level "%" — this migration must be applied as ONE READ COMMITTED transaction', v_iso
      USING ERRCODE = '55000',
            HINT = 'Every serialization claim in this file rests on a fresh per-statement snapshot: a lock is taken and the catalog is then re-read under it. At REPEATABLE READ or SERIALIZABLE this transaction''s snapshot predates the locks, so anything committed in between is invisible to every scan here and the install would assert a property that is already false. Apply the whole file as ONE READ COMMITTED transaction. Nothing has been written.';
  END IF;

  -- (3) NOT READ ONLY. Measured on this server version, `pg_current_xact_id()` succeeds in a
  --     READ ONLY transaction, so nothing later fails early on its own; without this the install
  --     would run its reads, pass its gates, and then abort halfway through on the first write.
  IF v_ro OPERATOR(pg_catalog.<>) 'off' THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 envelope: this transaction is READ ONLY — this migration creates roles, relations and grants and cannot run in it'
      USING ERRCODE = '55000',
            HINT = 'Apply this migration in a read-write transaction as the declared deploying role. Refusing here rather than partway through is the difference between a clean abort and an abort with half the unit''s DDL attempted. Nothing has been written.';
  END IF;
END $abc27_envelope$;

-- ── STAGE 7.4-B PREREQUISITE: THE SERVER MUST BE UTF8, CHECKED BEFORE ANY OBJECT IS TOUCHED ──
--
-- WHY THIS EXISTS. The owner-approved normalized apply contract (receipt d6790024…) specifies
-- Unicode semantics that PostgreSQL can only provide on a UTF8 server: the holiday label is
-- NFC-normalized and bounded in UTF-8 BYTES, and the canonical pre-image is a defined byte
-- grammar. `normalize(x, NFC)` RAISES on any non-UTF8 server — including for pure-ASCII input —
-- so on a SQL_ASCII database the approved rule is not merely untested, it is unexecutable.
--
-- WHY A REFUSAL AND NOT A FALLBACK. The obvious "fix" is to branch: normalize when the server is
-- UTF8, skip when it is not. That would make the canonical fingerprint a function of the
-- SERVER'S CONFIGURATION rather than of the reviewed intent — two databases would hash the same
-- accepted command differently, and a digest that depends on where it was computed is not a
-- digest. The second "fix", refusing non-NFC input instead of normalizing it, is a different
-- rule from the one the owner approved. Both are rejected here in favour of one closed refusal.
--
-- WHY IT IS FIRST AMONG THE UNIT'S OWN REFUSALS, AND WHY THAT IS TESTABLE. Exactly two statements
-- precede it: the search-path pin (a grammar-level utility command that resolves nothing and
-- cannot fail) and the environment guard (which reads three settings, writes nothing and passes on
-- any connection this migration may run on at all). It comes BEFORE §0's preflight, before
-- Domain-P discovery, and before the first CREATE/ALTER/GRANT/REVOKE of any kind, and it depends
-- on NO predecessor object — so on a conforming connection the encoding refusal is reachable, and
-- provably the first failure, in a bare `template0` database that carries none of this unit's
-- lineage. That is exactly how the suite proves it: a disposable SQL_ASCII database, the complete
-- migration bytes, this refusal, and zero D7 relation/function/trigger/role/grant residue
-- afterwards.
--
-- AND IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HARDENED AGAINST A FOREIGN SEARCH PATH, because the guard above is.
-- This block's `v_encoding text := …` is exactly the declaration a caller-planted DOMAIN named
-- `text` would capture, and measured on this server version it does: the domain's CHECK runs the
-- caller's function, and under a runner that commits per statement that write stays. Hardening
-- this one declaration would move the problem to the next unqualified name rather than solve it;
-- what solves it is refusing the path itself, once, before anything is resolved under it.
--
-- WHAT IT DOES NOT CLAIM. It says nothing about the hosted database's COLLATION. The disposable
-- local predecessor is deliberately `C`, for determinism; the contract's canonical ordering is
-- required to be typed or explicitly collation-independent precisely so that the hosted
-- database's default text collation is never part of what a fingerprint means.
DO $abc27_encoding$
DECLARE
  v_encoding text := current_setting('server_encoding');
BEGIN
  IF v_encoding IS DISTINCT FROM 'UTF8' THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: server_encoding is %, but this unit requires UTF8', v_encoding
      USING ERRCODE = '0A000',
            HINT = 'The approved normalized-intent contract normalizes holiday labels to NFC and bounds them in UTF-8 bytes; Unicode normalization is only available on a UTF8 server. This migration refuses rather than branching to a second canonical form whose fingerprint would depend on the server''s encoding.';
  END IF;
END $abc27_encoding$;


-- `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` and `ADD CONSTRAINT` guarded only on a constraint NAME are how a
-- migration silently inherits an object of unknown shape: the name matches, the migration reports
-- success, and every assertion downstream is then testing something nobody wrote. A pre-existing
-- object here means the environment is not what this migration was written against, and the
-- honest response is to stop.
DO $abc27_preflight$
DECLARE
  v_obj                   text;
  v_status_attnum         smallint;
  v_status_check_count    int;
  v_status_constraint_oid oid;
  v_actual_status_expr    text;
  v_expected_status_expr  text;
BEGIN
  -- The trusted resolution environment is already pinned by the top-level `SET LOCAL` ABOVE
  -- this block — before this block's own DECLARE was compiled, which is the property no
  -- in-block statement can provide (review round 4).

  FOREACH v_obj IN ARRAY ARRAY[
    'rebook_rounds', 'rebook_round_commands', 'rebook_round_command_children',
    -- D5 (owner-approved Option A): the immutable recipient universe and its provenance.
    'rebook_round_recipients', 'rebook_round_recipient_claim_sources',
    -- D7: target-scoped operation authority, the sole terminal decision authority, and the
    -- operator-visible incident evidence. Same refusal rule: this migration CREATES them and
    -- will not adopt an object of unknown shape.
    'rebook_round_operations', 'rebook_round_operation_targets',
    'rebook_round_transport_transitions',
    'rebook_round_recipient_decisions', 'rebook_round_materialization_incidents',
    -- Stage 7.4-A (receipt 87c0cd41): the one-use product-DML consumption receipt.
    'rebook_round_product_dml_consumptions'
  ] LOOP
    IF EXISTS (
      SELECT 1 FROM pg_class c JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = c.relnamespace
       WHERE n.nspname = 'public' AND c.relname = v_obj
    ) THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: public.% already exists — this migration creates it and will not adopt an object of unknown shape', v_obj
        USING HINT = 'Inspect the existing object. If it is a partial/aborted apply, drop it deliberately; do not re-run over it.';
    END IF;
  END LOOP;

  FOREACH v_obj IN ARRAY ARRAY[
    'rebook_round_id', 'rebook_member_open_materialized_at'
  ] LOOP
    IF EXISTS (
      SELECT 1 FROM information_schema.columns
       WHERE table_schema = 'public' AND table_name = 'cycles' AND column_name = v_obj
    ) THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: public.cycles.% already exists — refusing to adopt it', v_obj;
    END IF;
  END LOOP;

  FOREACH v_obj IN ARRAY ARRAY[
    'related_rebook_round_id', 'related_rebook_round_recipient_id'
  ] LOOP
    IF EXISTS (
      SELECT 1 FROM information_schema.columns
       WHERE table_schema = 'public' AND table_name = 'notification_outbox' AND column_name = v_obj
    ) THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: public.notification_outbox.% already exists — refusing to adopt it', v_obj;
    END IF;
  END LOOP;

  -- Stage 7.4-A (review): NO ROUTINE WEARING A D7 NAME CLASS MAY PRE-EXIST. `CREATE OR REPLACE`
  -- PRESERVES an existing function's ACL, this migration's REVOKEs name only PUBLIC and the three
  -- runtime roots, and the ownership pass rewrites only the OWNER'S ACL entries — so a pre-seeded
  -- routine under a D7 name could carry a hostile EXECUTE grant straight through the install.
  -- Every routine this unit MINTS is new under one of the name classes below; the predecessor
  -- chain defines none of them (the rebook GROUP-claim functions use different names, and both
  -- the notification-lineage functions this migration legitimately replaces — the resolver hook,
  -- `enqueue_notification` — and the four per-cycle notice RPCs that §10a retires
  -- signature-preserving are DELIBERATELY outside these classes: replacement is their contract,
  -- and §10c's owner-only ACL closure is what proves their final grants). A match here is
  -- therefore an adoption attempt, and adoption is refused.
  IF EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM pg_proc p JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = p.pronamespace
     WHERE n.nspname = 'public'
       AND (p.proname LIKE 'rebook\_round\_%' OR p.proname LIKE 'rebook\_member\_open\_%'
            OR p.proname LIKE 'abc27\_%' OR p.proname LIKE 'guard\_rebook\_%'
            OR p.proname IN ('guard_cycle_round_relation', 'guard_cycle_marker_capability',
                             -- The notification-side routines this unit MINTS (review round 2):
                             -- none of these exists in the predecessor chain, so a match is the
                             -- same adoption attempt the classes above refuse. The resolver hook
                             -- and enqueue_notification stay outside — replacement is their
                             -- contract.
                             'notification_outbox_round_ref_guard',
                             'guard_notification_outbox_no_armed_commit',
                             'guard_notification_event_type_authority',
                             'notif_caller_terminal_skip_reasons'))
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: a routine wearing a D7 name class already exists — this migration creates every one of them and will not adopt or replace a pre-existing body or its ACL'
      USING HINT = 'Inspect the existing routine. If it is a partial/aborted apply, drop it deliberately; do not re-run over it.';
  END IF;

  -- AND THE SAME REFUSAL FOR THE TRIGGERS THIS UNIT MINTS ON SHARED PREDECESSOR RELATIONS
  -- (review round 2): their `DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS` idiom is idempotency ritual, not adoption
  -- permission — a same-named pre-existing trigger would be silently dropped and replaced, its
  -- semantics never reviewed. Scoped to the EXACT (relation, name) pairs this unit creates
  -- (round 3): trigger names are relation-local, and an unrelated table elsewhere carrying one
  -- of these names is neither adoptable nor droppable by this migration and must not abort it.
  -- The D7-table triggers cannot pre-exist because their tables are refused above.
  IF EXISTS (
    SELECT 1
      FROM (VALUES
        ('public.notification_event_types', 'trg_guard_notification_event_type_authority'),
        ('public.notification_outbox',      'trg_notification_outbox_round_ref_guard'),
        ('public.notification_outbox',      'trg_notification_outbox_no_armed_commit'),
        ('public.cycles',                   'trg_guard_cycle_round_relation'),
        ('public.cycles',                   'trg_guard_cycle_zz_marker_capability')
      ) AS minted(rel, tgname)
      JOIN pg_trigger t
        ON t.tgrelid = to_regclass(minted.rel)
       AND t.tgname = minted.tgname
       AND NOT t.tgisinternal
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: a trigger wearing a D7 name already exists on its exact relation — this migration creates every one of them and will not drop and replace a pre-existing one'
      USING HINT = 'Inspect the existing trigger. If it is a partial/aborted apply, drop it deliberately; do not re-run over it.';
  END IF;

  -- D5: the snapshot's XOR constraint and the outbox's coherence CHECK both use num_nonnulls.
  IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_proc p JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = p.pronamespace
                  WHERE n.nspname = 'pg_catalog' AND p.proname = 'num_nonnulls') THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: pg_catalog.num_nonnulls is required for the recipient-snapshot subject constraints';
  END IF;

  -- D7 checkpoint 1: eligibility hard-codes pending|expired as the only outstanding states. Pin
  -- the predecessor's enforcing CHECK structurally, before this migration creates or alters any
  -- persistent object. A text search over pg_get_constraintdef is not closed-world evidence: a
  -- sixth literal, an extra OR, a second competing CHECK, NOT VALID or NO INHERIT could all pass.
  SELECT a.attnum
    INTO v_status_attnum
    FROM pg_attribute a
   WHERE a.attrelid = to_regclass('public.slot_priority_claims')
     AND a.attname = 'status'
     AND a.attnum > 0
     AND NOT a.attisdropped;
  IF v_status_attnum IS NULL THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: public.slot_priority_claims.status is missing';
  END IF;

  SELECT count(*), (array_agg(con.oid ORDER BY con.oid))[1]
    INTO v_status_check_count, v_status_constraint_oid
    FROM pg_constraint con
   WHERE con.conrelid = 'public.slot_priority_claims'::regclass
     AND con.contype = 'c'
     AND v_status_attnum = ANY (con.conkey);
  IF v_status_check_count <> 1 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: public.slot_priority_claims.status must have exactly one CHECK, found %', v_status_check_count;
  END IF;
  IF NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM pg_constraint con
     WHERE con.oid = v_status_constraint_oid
       AND con.conkey = ARRAY[v_status_attnum]::smallint[]
       AND con.convalidated
       AND NOT con.connoinherit
       AND con.conislocal
       AND con.conparentid = 0
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: the sole public.slot_priority_claims.status CHECK must be validated, inheritable, local and single-column';
  END IF;

  -- THE COLUMN'S COMPLETE LOAD-BEARING SHAPE, not just its CHECK (Stage 7.4-A review rounds
  -- 3–4). A SQL CHECK accepts NULL, so with NOT NULL dropped a NULL-status claim is legal — and
  -- the freeze would then refuse its round as "unreadable" on every attempt. And the shape is
  -- more than nullability: `pg_attrdef` also stores GENERATED expressions, so an
  -- ordinary-looking default pin would accept a `GENERATED ALWAYS AS ('pending') STORED` column
  -- that refuses every ordinary transition, and a nondeterministic per-column collation could
  -- admit case variants into the purportedly closed lowercase vocabulary. The shipped shape is
  -- an ordinary, non-generated, non-identity, default-collated base `text` column, NOT NULL,
  -- with the plain DEFAULT 'pending'; anything else is a different product design.
  IF NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1
      FROM pg_attribute a
      JOIN pg_attrdef d ON d.adrelid = a.attrelid AND d.adnum = a.attnum
     WHERE a.attrelid = to_regclass('public.slot_priority_claims')
       AND a.attnum = v_status_attnum
       AND a.attnotnull
       AND a.attgenerated = ''
       AND a.attidentity = ''
       AND a.atttypid = 'text'::regtype
       AND a.atttypmod = -1
       AND a.attcollation = (SELECT t.typcollation FROM pg_type t WHERE t.oid = 'text'::regtype)
       AND pg_get_expr(d.adbin, d.adrelid) = '''pending''::text'
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: public.slot_priority_claims.status must be an ordinary non-generated default-collated base text column, NOT NULL, with DEFAULT ''pending'' — the shipped load-bearing shape';
  END IF;

  EXECUTE $sql$
    CREATE TEMP TABLE pg_temp.abc27_expected_claim_status (
      status text CONSTRAINT abc27_expected_claim_status_check
        CHECK (status IN ('pending','claimed','declined','expired','released'))
    ) ON COMMIT DROP
  $sql$;
  SELECT pg_get_expr(con.conbin, con.conrelid, false)
    INTO v_expected_status_expr
    FROM pg_constraint con
   WHERE con.conrelid = 'pg_temp.abc27_expected_claim_status'::regclass
     AND con.contype = 'c';
  SELECT pg_get_expr(con.conbin, con.conrelid, false)
    INTO v_actual_status_expr
    FROM pg_constraint con
   WHERE con.oid = v_status_constraint_oid;
  IF v_actual_status_expr IS DISTINCT FROM v_expected_status_expr THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: public.slot_priority_claims.status CHECK is not the closed five-value vocabulary (actual %, expected %)',
      v_actual_status_expr, v_expected_status_expr;
  END IF;

  -- D7 REFUSE-TO-ADOPT THE EVENT ROW. `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (key) DO UPDATE` would silently
  -- overwrite a pre-existing catalog row — including one an operator or an aborted apply left
  -- behind — and every enforcement predicate in this unit is read FROM that row. A pre-existing
  -- row means the environment is not what this migration was written against, whether or not its
  -- values happen to match today. §6 therefore uses a bare INSERT, which this check makes safe.
  IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.notification_event_types WHERE key = 'rebook_member_open_player') THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: notification_event_types.rebook_member_open_player already exists — refusing to adopt or overwrite a security-bearing catalog row'
      USING HINT = 'Inspect the existing row. If it is a partial/aborted apply, remove it deliberately; do not re-run over it.';
  END IF;

  -- D7: the outbox columns this unit adds. Same refusal rule as the round/recipient columns.
  FOREACH v_obj IN ARRAY ARRAY[
    'transport_state', 'first_dispatch_at', 'uncertainty_deadline_at',
    'leased_from_state', 'lease_generation', 'dispatch_authorized_generation',
    'request_hash', 'provider_idempotency_key', 'pending_delete_grant_id'
  ] LOOP
    IF EXISTS (
      SELECT 1 FROM information_schema.columns
       WHERE table_schema = 'public' AND table_name = 'notification_outbox' AND column_name = v_obj
    ) THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: public.notification_outbox.% already exists — refusing to adopt it', v_obj;
    END IF;
  END LOOP;

  -- The dependencies this migration hard-requires. Failing here names the missing thing; failing
  -- later would produce an error about a column nobody can place.
  IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_proc p JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = p.pronamespace
                  WHERE n.nspname = 'pg_catalog' AND p.proname = 'sha256') THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: pg_catalog.sha256(bytea) is required (PostgreSQL 11+) for server-derived command fingerprints';
  END IF;
  IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_class c JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = c.relnamespace
                  WHERE n.nspname = 'public' AND c.relname = 'notification_outbox') THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: public.notification_outbox is required — this unit reuses it and never forks a second retry table';
  END IF;
END $abc27_preflight$;

-- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- §0a. ABORT-FIRST BASELINE FOR THE SHARED `academy_managers` RLS POLICIES.
-- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- WHY THIS RUNS BEFORE ANY D7 OBJECT EXISTS. Further down, this unit narrows two PRE-EXISTING
-- policies on a SHARED table from PUBLIC applicability to `authenticated`. That is a change to
-- who can read manager rows across the whole product, so it may only proceed against the exact
-- baseline it was reviewed against. Running the check here means a drifted baseline aborts the
-- transaction while the database still contains no D7 object at all.
--
-- WHY THE COMPARISON IS AGAINST `pg_get_expr`, NOT THE MIGRATION SOURCE. The catalog normalizes a
-- policy predicate: the shipped source reads
--   `academy_profile_id IN (SELECT get_user_academy_ids(auth.uid()))`
-- while the catalog stores
--   `(academy_profile_id IN ( SELECT get_user_academy_ids(auth.uid()) AS get_user_academy_ids))`.
-- A regex or substring test over source text would therefore "pass" against a predicate that is
-- not the one in force. Only the normalized expression proves what the database will evaluate.
--
-- ALTER POLICY MUST NEVER SILENTLY NORMALIZE A DRIFT. If an operator has edited either policy, or
-- added any other SELECT policy that could apply to this unit's owner, the correct response is to
-- stop and let a human decide — not to overwrite their change.
--
-- ── AND THIS COPY OF THE BASELINE IS EXPLICITLY NOT THE AUTHORITATIVE ONE ────────────────────
--
-- WHAT IT IS. A fast-fail diagnostic, kept here because failing at the top of the file — before a
-- role exists, before a lock is taken, before anything queues behind this transaction — is the
-- cheapest and clearest way to tell an operator that their database is not the one this unit was
-- reviewed against. Nothing downstream is entitled to rely on it.
--
-- WHY IT CANNOT BE THE AUTHORITY, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. This block holds NO lock on
-- `public.academy_managers` when it finishes. Its heaviest reader is `pg_get_expr`, and measured on
-- this server version `pg_get_expr_worker` opens the target relation, renders the expression and
-- RELEASES its `AccessShareLock` before returning — so the instant this block ends, any owner of
-- that table may commit `CREATE POLICY`, `ALTER POLICY` or `DROP POLICY` on it, and every
-- conclusion drawn here is stale with no way to notice. A second unlocked scan later would narrow
-- that window and could never close it; this file's own doctrine says so, and this comment is not
-- an exception to it.
--
-- WHERE THE AUTHORITY ACTUALLY LIVES. The final re-verification block re-runs these same three
-- assertions AFTER acquiring the canonical `SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE` set on this table and the nine
-- Domain-P relations, in the SAME statement as the policy DDL they protect. That is the copy whose
-- result is load-bearing; this one exists to fail early and to fail cheaply.
DO $abc27_policy_baseline$
DECLARE
  v_qual_managers CONSTANT text :=
    '(academy_profile_id IN ( SELECT get_user_academy_ids(auth.uid()) AS get_user_academy_ids))';
  v_qual_admins   CONSTANT text := 'is_admin(auth.uid())';
  v_owner         CONSTANT name := 'padeltrainer_abc27_owner';
  v_n             int;
BEGIN
  -- The table itself: RLS on, FORCE off, and NOT owned by this unit's operator owner. A D7-owned
  -- shared table would make the owner exempt from RLS and silently defeat the whole composition.
  IF NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM pg_class c JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = c.relnamespace
     WHERE n.nspname='public' AND c.relname='academy_managers'
       AND c.relrowsecurity AND NOT c.relforcerowsecurity
       AND c.relowner::regrole::name <> v_owner
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: public.academy_managers must have RLS enabled, FORCE row security off, and an owner other than %', v_owner
      USING HINT = 'This unit narrows that table''s SELECT policies. It refuses to do so against an unexpected RLS or ownership baseline.';
  END IF;

  -- EXACTLY the two known SELECT policies, each permissive, each applying to PUBLIC (polroles
  -- containing role 0), each carrying its exact normalized predicate.
  IF NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM pg_policy p
     WHERE p.polrelid = 'public.academy_managers'::regclass
       AND p.polname = 'Academy managers can view their managers'
       AND p.polcmd = 'r' AND p.polpermissive
       AND p.polroles = ARRAY[0::oid]
       AND pg_get_expr(p.polqual, p.polrelid) = v_qual_managers
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: the "Academy managers can view their managers" policy is not the exact reviewed baseline (command/permissiveness/PUBLIC role set/qual)'
      USING HINT = 'Refusing to ALTER a policy whose predicate or audience has drifted from the reviewed one.';
  END IF;

  IF NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM pg_policy p
     WHERE p.polrelid = 'public.academy_managers'::regclass
       AND p.polname = 'Admins can view all academy managers'
       AND p.polcmd = 'r' AND p.polpermissive
       AND p.polroles = ARRAY[0::oid]
       AND pg_get_expr(p.polqual, p.polrelid) = v_qual_admins
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: the "Admins can view all academy managers" policy is not the exact reviewed baseline (command/permissiveness/PUBLIC role set/qual)'
      USING HINT = 'Refusing to ALTER a policy whose predicate or audience has drifted from the reviewed one.';
  END IF;

  -- NO OTHER SELECT POLICY MAY EXIST on this table. An unexpected extra permissive SELECT policy
  -- would be OR-combined into whatever role set it names — including, potentially, this unit's
  -- owner — quietly widening the exact-self-row read this composition is built around.
  SELECT count(*) INTO v_n
    FROM pg_policy p
   WHERE p.polrelid = 'public.academy_managers'::regclass
     AND p.polcmd IN ('r','*');
  IF v_n <> 2 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 preflight: public.academy_managers has % SELECT-applicable policies, expected exactly the 2 reviewed ones', v_n
      USING HINT = 'An additional SELECT policy could apply to the D7 operator owner and widen its read beyond its own membership row.';
  END IF;
END $abc27_policy_baseline$;

-- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- §0b. F-B — THE DEDICATED NOLOGIN OPERATOR OWNER.
-- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- WHY A SEPARATE OWNER AT ALL. The operator wrappers are SECURITY DEFINER, so whoever owns them
-- is the authority every operator command actually runs as. Owning them as `postgres` would mean
-- an operator RPC executes with superuser rights and only the wrapper body stands between a
-- request and the entire database. A dedicated owner makes the blast radius of a wrapper bug the
-- set of privileges GRANTED TO THIS ROLE below and nothing else granted to it. The bound is on
-- grants to the role, and it is stated that way deliberately: privileges held by PUBLIC, and the
-- defaults a NULL ACL column implies, remain effective for this role as they are for every other
-- one. Those are ambient facts of the database, identical whether this block mints a fresh owner
-- or adopts an existing role, so they are create/adopt-neutral rather than a hole in the claim —
-- and §0b's adoption arms below refuse anything granted specifically TO a candidate.
--
-- WHY NOLOGIN, AND WHY NO MEMBERSHIP IN EITHER DIRECTION. `NOLOGIN` means no credential can
-- authenticate as this role directly. The membership rules are the load-bearing half: if a
-- runtime role were a member of the owner, `SET ROLE` would hand a request the owner's authority
-- outright; if the owner were a member of a runtime role, it would inherit that role's reach.
-- §10c proves both directions are absent, recursively, and that no permitted `SET ROLE` path
-- exists from `anon`, `authenticated` or `service_role`.
--
-- WHY NOINHERIT, AND WHAT IT IS AND IS NOT. `rolinherit = false` supplies the DEFAULT inheritance
-- for edges created later; it is not a ceiling. Since PostgreSQL 16 a grant may say
-- `GRANT … WITH INHERIT TRUE` and that edge inherits regardless of the member's `rolinherit`. So
-- NOINHERIT narrows the accident, but the load-bearing control is the one below and in §10c: ZERO
-- membership edges, in either direction, with any role — a role on no edge inherits from nothing
-- no matter how a future grant is spelled.
--
-- BYPASSRLS IS DELIBERATELY NOT SET. It is not a SQL privilege and would not grant a single
-- table right; what it would do is silently disable row-level security for every statement the
-- definer runs, which is the opposite of least privilege. The owner instead receives exact table
-- privileges below, and the wrappers reach tenant data only through their private cores.
DO $abc27_owner$
DECLARE
  v_owner CONSTANT name := 'padeltrainer_abc27_owner';
  -- Scratch for the adopted-owner privilege-surface scan below. Untouched on the create path:
  -- a role this block mints cannot already be somebody's grantee.
  v_acl_oid   oid;
  v_acl_cat   name;
  v_acl_col   name;
  v_acl_privs text[];
  v_acl_found text[] := ARRAY[]::text[];
  -- Scratch for the adopted-owner shared-dependency ownership scan below. Same reasoning: a role
  -- this block mints cannot already own anything.
  v_own_found text;
  -- Scratch for the adopted-owner policy-audience scan below. Same reasoning once more: a role
  -- this block mints cannot already be named in somebody else's policy.
  v_pol_found text;
  -- Option A: the backends that hold or await an owner-targeting object lock on the candidate at
  -- the moment the audience check runs. Untouched on the create path, which takes no catalog lock
  -- and needs none — see the create branch's own note below.
  v_pol_lock_pids text;
BEGIN
  IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = v_owner) THEN
    EXECUTE format(
      'CREATE ROLE %I NOLOGIN NOINHERIT NOBYPASSRLS NOSUPERUSER NOCREATEDB NOCREATEROLE NOREPLICATION',
      v_owner);
    -- THE ROLE THIS BRANCH MINTS IS PROVABLY RACE-FREE, WHICH IS WHY THIS BRANCH TAKES NO CATALOG
    -- LOCK. An uncommitted `CREATE ROLE` is invisible to every other session — measured, a
    -- concurrent backend reads zero matching `pg_roles` rows and a policy naming this role fails
    -- `42704` — so no third party can name this candidate as a policy audience before this
    -- transaction commits. The adoption branch below has the opposite problem (the role is already
    -- globally visible) and that is exactly where the catalog serialization lives.
    --
    -- THAT ARGUMENT IS ABOUT THE ROLE, AND ABOUT NOTHING ELSE. This branch also goes on to run the
    -- shared `academy_managers` composition and to consume the nine-relation Domain-P policy
    -- digest, and both of those are table-scoped surfaces a concurrent owner can change on this
    -- path exactly as on the adoption one. Those are serialized by the canonical product-table lock
    -- set the final block takes on BOTH paths — not here, and not by this branch's invisibility
    -- argument, which never covered them.
    --
    -- THIS BRANCH RECORDS NOTHING THAT OUTLIVES ITS TRANSACTION, AND THAT IS THE CHANGE. It does
    -- record one thing — the sentinel described below — but nothing of it survives COMMIT, so the
    -- installed database is left exactly as it would be had the branch recorded nothing. An earlier
    -- revision instead took a
    -- transaction-scoped ADVISORY LOCK here, so the final re-verification could tell this path
    -- apart from the adoption path and from a runner that split the file. That witness is DELETED,
    -- because it could not do the job it was given, for four independent reasons:
    --
    --   * `pg_locks` carries no scope column. Measured on this server version, the two-argument
    --     SESSION form `pg_advisory_lock(int4, int4)` and the transaction form
    --     `pg_advisory_xact_lock(int4, int4)` produce rows identical in `locktype`, `classid`,
    --     `objid` and `objsubid`, and the witness read could constrain neither scope (there is
    --     nothing to constrain) nor mode.
    --   * A session-scoped advisory key outlives its transaction on the same backend. A wrapper
    --     script or a pooled connection that had taken `(20261118, 120000)` earlier therefore made
    --     the witness TRUE in a transaction where this branch never ran.
    --   * The advisory namespace is PUBLIC-callable and the key was this file's own name, so it was
    --     predictable by anyone who could read the migration; any principal holding CONNECT could
    --     forge presence on a shared backend, or hold the key and block the un-timeboxed
    --     acquisition indefinitely.
    --   * Lock presence encodes "held now". It carries no happens-before semantics whatsoever, so
    --     it could never have meant "this transaction created this role" — which is the only thing
    --     the dispatch below actually needs to know.
    --
    -- WHAT REPLACES IT IS A TRANSACTION-LOCAL SENTINEL, CREATED HERE, ONE STATEMENT AFTER THE ROLE
    -- (owner receipt `…WINDOW_HARDENING_AND_FINAL_LOOP_V1`). It is an `ON COMMIT DROP` temporary
    -- relation carrying the exact role identity this branch minted, and the final re-verification
    -- dispatches on its presence.
    --
    -- WHY A TEMPORARY RELATION AND NOT A PROPERTY OF THE ROLE TUPLE. The previous revision read the
    -- created role's own `pg_authid` tuple and asked whether its writer was current and
    -- uncommitted. That is right in every ordinary case and wrong in one that cannot be ruled out
    -- by more arithmetic: a tuple header carries the RAW 32-bit id, and those bits survive
    -- freezing, so after a full transaction-id cycle an old committed role can present exactly the
    -- low 32 bits this transaction is using. No amount of epoch reconstruction recovers the epoch a
    -- frozen tuple was originally written in. The sentinel has no such failure mode because it is
    -- not derived from an id at all: it either exists in this session's temporary schema right now
    -- or it does not.
    --
    -- WHAT MAKES IT UNFORGEABLE IN THE SHAPES THAT MATTER — which is a claim about OTHER SESSIONS
    -- and about ACCIDENTS, not about this session acting against itself. Measured on this server
    -- version:
    --
    --   * `ON COMMIT DROP` means it CANNOT outlive the transaction that made it. A runner that
    --     commits between §0b and the final block — the split-run shape — arrives with the relation
    --     already gone, and is therefore expected to be refused: expected rather than proven,
    --     because the same session could have made another relation of that name in the meantime.
    --     Measured: created in an autocommit statement, it is absent the moment that statement's
    --     implicit transaction ends.
    --   * A temporary relation is SESSION-local, so no other backend can see it or create one on
    --     this backend's behalf. Measured: a second session reads it as absent while this one holds
    --     it. This is the property the dispatch actually rests on, and it is the strongest one
    --     available: what remains forgeable is confined to the very session the executor contract
    --     already places inside the trust boundary.
    --   * It survives `RELEASE SAVEPOINT`, at any nesting depth, which is what a conforming
    --     executor that wraps the apply — or wraps every statement — actually does. That is the
    --     case the id comparison got wrong, and it is measured in both shapes.
    --   * `ROLLBACK` removes it, together with the role it names, so the two facts cannot disagree.
    --   * There is deliberately no `IF NOT EXISTS`. A relation of this name already present in the
    --     session makes this statement fail with `42P07` and the install stops, rather than this
    --     branch adopting somebody else's object as its own evidence.
    --
    -- WHAT IT IS NOT. It is not an advisory lock, not a setting at any scope, and not a persistent
    -- object: nothing of it survives `COMMIT`, so the installed database contains no trace of it and
    -- the unit's own manifests never see it. The residual is stated rather than hidden: an operator
    -- who deliberately created a same-named temporary relation earlier in THIS session, without
    -- `ON COMMIT DROP`, would be believed — but that operator is the declared executor, and an
    -- executor prepared to plant evidence against itself is outside every model in this file.
    EXECUTE format(
      'CREATE TEMP TABLE abc27_create_sentinel (owner pg_catalog.name NOT NULL) ON COMMIT DROP');
    EXECUTE format(
      'INSERT INTO pg_temp.abc27_create_sentinel(owner) VALUES (%L)', v_owner);
    --
    -- IT IS A DISPATCH FACT, AND IT IS NOT AN ATOMICITY PROOF. It says which branch ran and whether
    -- that branch's work is still uncommitted; it says nothing about whether this file will be
    -- applied whole, and nothing here can. Whole-file atomicity is the declared executor's
    -- contract, verified by the deployment runbook — see the environment guard at the top of this
    -- file, which is careful to claim only what a connection can be asked about right now.
    --
    -- THE ADOPTION BRANCH BELOW NEEDS NO EQUIVALENT, because its witness is the serialization lock
    -- itself: SQL exposes no session-scoped relation locks, so an own-pid granted
    -- `ShareRowExclusiveLock` row on `pg_catalog.pg_policy` can only mean this transaction holds the
    -- lock — proving the branch and the protection in one read.
  ELSE
    -- ── THE ADOPTION PATH IS SOUND ONLY UNDER READ COMMITTED, SO THE LEVEL IS PROVED, NOT ASSUMED.
    --
    -- WHAT THE REST OF THIS BRANCH RESTS ON. The whole serialization argument is "lock the
    -- catalog, THEN scan it, and the scan sees everything committed before the lock". That is a
    -- property of READ COMMITTED alone, where each statement takes a FRESH snapshot. It was never
    -- required anywhere, and a runner is free to open the transaction at any level.
    --
    -- AND THE BYPASS IS REAL, MEASURED ON THIS SERVER VERSION, NOT HYPOTHETICAL. Under
    -- `REPEATABLE READ` or `SERIALIZABLE` the transaction's snapshot is fixed by its FIRST
    -- statement — and §0 above already reads catalogs and one product relation, so the snapshot is
    -- taken long before this branch begins. A third party may then commit
    -- `CREATE POLICY … TO <candidate>` after that snapshot and before this transaction reaches the
    -- lock below: the writer's transient catalog lock and its `pg_authid` object lock are both
    -- gone by the time the probe runs, so the probe sees nothing, and BOTH `pg_shdepend` scans —
    -- §0b's and the final re-verification's — read the OLD snapshot and see nothing either. The
    -- install then asserts an empty audience while a committed policy names the candidate.
    -- Measured, that exact sequence reproduces: post-lock scan reads 0 rows with 0 object locks
    -- under `REPEATABLE READ`, and reads the committed policy under `READ COMMITTED`.
    --
    -- SO THE LEVEL IS A PRECONDITION, AND IT IS REFUSED LOUDLY RATHER THAN DEGRADED QUIETLY.
    -- There is no weaker fallback to take: a snapshot that cannot be refreshed cannot be made to
    -- see a concurrent commit by locking harder. The gate is the FIRST executable statement of
    -- this branch — before any adoption read — so a wrong level costs nothing and reads nothing.
    -- THIS BRANCH-LOCAL GATE IS NOT THE ONLY ONE ANY MORE, AND SAYING SO MATTERS. The environment
    -- guard at the top of this file refuses a non-`READ COMMITTED` transaction on EVERY path,
    -- before the first side effect, so in any whole-file run this restatement is unreachable and
    -- the create branch is gated exactly as this one is. It is kept because it is the gate that
    -- protects the adoption arms specifically, and because executing this block on its own — which
    -- the suite does — reaches it where the envelope cannot.
    IF pg_catalog.current_setting('transaction_isolation') <> 'read committed' THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27: this transaction is running at isolation level "%" — refusing to adopt an owner whose policy audience cannot be re-read after the serialization lock',
        pg_catalog.current_setting('transaction_isolation')
        USING ERRCODE = '55000',
              HINT = 'Adoption proves an empty policy audience by locking pg_catalog.pg_policy and then re-reading the catalog. Only READ COMMITTED takes a fresh snapshot per statement, so only there does the post-lock read see everything committed before the lock; at REPEATABLE READ or SERIALIZABLE the transaction''s snapshot predates the lock and a policy committed in between is invisible to every scan in this file. Apply this migration as ONE READ COMMITTED transaction. Nothing has been written.';
    END IF;

    -- ADOPTION IS REFUSED UNLESS THE EXISTING ROLE IS ALREADY EXACTLY THIS SHAPE. A role wearing
    -- this name that can log in, bypass RLS, create roles or is superuser is not an owner — it is
    -- an escalation path wearing an owner's name, and silently ALTERing somebody else's role is
    -- worse than failing. This arm is where the ATTRIBUTES are first proved: §10c re-proves the
    -- MEMBERSHIP topology and the function/relation surfaces after install, and observes
    -- superuser/BYPASSRLS on the authority-table owner without refusing on it. This is not the only
    -- read of the attribute set, though: the final re-verification reads it once more as a
    -- tripwire, under the product locks, immediately before the composition — narrowing the window
    -- rather than closing it, which is what that block says about it in those words.
    IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = v_owner
                 AND (rolcanlogin OR rolsuper OR rolbypassrls OR rolcreaterole
                      OR rolcreatedb OR rolreplication OR rolinherit)) THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27: role % already exists with login/superuser/bypassrls/inherit/creator/replication attributes — refusing to adopt it as the F-B operator owner', v_owner
        USING HINT = 'Inspect the existing role deliberately. This migration will not weaken or repurpose a role it did not create.';
    END IF;

    -- AN ADOPTED OWNER MUST OWN NOTHING, ANYWHERE IN THIS DATABASE (Stage 7.4-A review). The
    -- residue and manifest proofs close over the objects THIS migration gives Domain A; a role
    -- adopted with a pre-seeded surface — a SECURITY DEFINER routine in a side schema, a definer
    -- view, an owned schema — would join the domain silently, outside every projection, and this
    -- unit's grants (`cycles` above all) would activate it. Owner adoption is therefore legal
    -- only for a role with a completely empty object surface: no relation of any kind, no
    -- routine, no schema, in any namespace.
    IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_class c
                WHERE c.relowner = (SELECT oid FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = v_owner))
       OR EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_proc p
                   WHERE p.proowner = (SELECT oid FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = v_owner))
       OR EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_namespace ns
                   WHERE ns.nspowner = (SELECT oid FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = v_owner)) THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27: role % already owns relations, routines or schemas — refusing to adopt a non-empty owner as the F-B operator owner', v_owner
        USING HINT = 'Every Domain-A object must be minted by this migration and appear in its canonical manifest. A pre-seeded surface cannot be reviewed into the domain.';
    END IF;

    -- OWNERSHIP IS NOT THREE CATALOGS — IT IS EVERY CATALOG THE SERVER SAYS IT IS. The arm above
    -- defines "owns nothing" as `pg_class` + `pg_proc` + `pg_namespace`. Those are three of the 23
    -- catalog columns `pg_get_catalog_foreign_keys()` reports as referencing `pg_authid` in an
    -- owner role on PostgreSQL 18. Ownership is also not a privilege: it never has to appear as an
    -- ACL entry, so the privilege-surface arm below — which keys on the GRANTEE and excludes
    -- `grantor = grantee` — cannot see it either. A candidate owning a database, a large object, a
    -- standalone type, a publication or a tablespace passes every arm above and arrives carrying
    -- authority this unit never reviewed, which a SECURITY DEFINER wrapper then inherits whole.
    --
    -- WHY A SHARED-DEPENDENCY SCAN RATHER THAN A 23-COLUMN LOOP. `pg_shdepend` is not a list of
    -- classes this file has to keep current: PostgreSQL records an owner dependency for every
    -- class that has an owner, so a class this migration never heard of — or one a later major
    -- version adds — is covered without editing this file. That is the same "ask the running
    -- server" doctrine the privilege-surface arm below already uses for its ACL inventory.
    -- Measured on this server version: a single candidate handed one object of each ownable kind
    -- records `'o'` rows under NINETEEN distinct classes, none of them hand-listed here.
    --
    -- `deptype` CLASSIFIES THE SIGNAL, AND EVERY VALUE HAS EXACTLY ONE OWNING ARM. On
    -- PostgreSQL 18 this catalog uses five flavors: ownership `'o'`, ACL grants `'a'`, the
    -- initial ACLs initdb or an extension installed `'i'`, RLS-policy audiences `'r'`, and
    -- tablespace dependencies `'t'`.
    --
    -- THERE IS NO `'p'`, AND AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS COMMENT SAID THERE WAS. It described
    -- `'p'` as "pinned system roles". Measured on this server version: no `'p'` row exists at
    -- all, and the pinned `pg_*` roles record ZERO `pg_shdepend` rows of ANY deptype — pinned
    -- objects stopped being recorded here, so there is no pin flavor for this scan to skip and
    -- no arm below owns one. Mapped onto this block, with no value left implied:
    --   'o' — THIS arm. Only ownership matches it, and it matches nothing else.
    --   'a' — the privilege-surface arm below. That arm does not read this catalog at all: it
    --         expands the ACL columns directly, so it sees the same grants WITH the object
    --         identity attached, which is what its refusal message needs.
    --   'r' — the POLICY-AUDIENCE arm immediately below this one. An audience is neither an
    --         aclitem nor an ownership, so NO other arm here can see it: not the attribute arm,
    --         not either ownership arm, not the privilege-surface arm. That is precisely why it
    --         needs an arm of its own rather than a mention in this comment.
    --   'i' — the initial ACLs initdb or an extension installed. Not authority a candidate
    --         acquired, and not refused.
    --   't' — a dependency on a TABLESPACE rather than on a role. It cannot reach this scan in
    --         any case: every arm here filters `refclassid = pg_authid`, and a tablespace
    --         dependency is recorded against `pg_tablespace`.
    -- Directly witnessed on this server version: `'o'`, `'a'` and `'r'`, each produced from a
    -- planted object of its own kind. `'i'` and `'t'` are named because the taxonomy must leave
    -- no value implied, not because this file has reproduced one.
    -- MEMBERSHIP IS NOT IN THIS CATALOG AT ALL, AND THIS COMMENT DOES NOT PRETEND IT IS. Measured
    -- on this server version: a membership-only role records NO `pg_shdepend` row of ANY deptype,
    -- so classification by deptype cannot reach it and does not claim to. The zero-edges arm
    -- further below is what closes that shape, by reading `pg_auth_members` directly.
    --
    -- THE DISCRIMINATION IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. With one candidate named as a policy audience
    -- and four control roles alongside it, the scoped `'r'` count reads: policy audience 2,
    -- ACL-grantee-only 0, membership-only 0, group role 0, pristine role 0. The `'o'` scan in this
    -- arm reads 0 for every one of the five — the policy audience INCLUDED — which is exactly why
    -- the next arm has to exist and cannot be folded into this one.
    --
    -- AND THE CONSEQUENCE IS NOT COSMETIC. Measured: a policy-audience candidate and a freshly
    -- minted role hold `SELECT` on the same table identically, through the same PUBLIC grant, yet
    -- under RLS the policy-audience candidate reads FIVE rows where the fresh role reads ZERO.
    -- That is the whole create/adopt asymmetry in one measurement: minting an owner cannot inherit
    -- a policy, adopting one can, and at every point this block could otherwise look the two roles
    -- are indistinguishable.
    --
    -- THE DATABASE CASE IS COVERED WITHOUT A SPECIAL CASE, AND IT IS THE SHARPEST ONE. A candidate
    -- owning a database records an `'o'` row with `dbid = 0`, so the generic scan catches it and
    -- no `pg_database` arm exists or is needed anywhere in this block. It matters because such a
    -- candidate holds `pg_database_owner` authority in that database through NO `pg_auth_members`
    -- edge at all — measured: `pg_has_role(candidate,'pg_database_owner','USAGE')` is true with
    -- zero rows in `pg_auth_members` — which is precisely why the membership arm below, on its
    -- own, cannot close it.
    --
    -- THE SCOPE IS THIS DATABASE PLUS SHARED OBJECTS, AND THAT BOUND IS DISCLOSED, NOT OMITTED.
    -- `pg_shdepend` is a shared catalog: it sees every database in the cluster. An unscoped scan
    -- would refuse a perfectly legitimate install into a SECOND database of this cluster whenever
    -- the candidate already owns this unit's surface in the first — which is the evidence suite's
    -- own topology, and would make this migration installable exactly once per cluster. So the
    -- scan is bounded to `dbid = 0` (cluster-shared objects) plus this database's OID. READ THAT
    -- BOUND EXACTLY, BECAUSE THE OBVIOUS SHORTHAND FOR IT IS FALSE. Ownership of another DATABASE
    -- is not per-database at all: it is recorded `dbid = 0`, it is therefore inside this scope,
    -- and it IS refused here — the evidence suite's database-ownership control plants exactly that
    -- and pins this arm's message. What is NOT a refusal condition is ownership of a PER-DATABASE
    -- object that lives in a different database of this cluster. PostgreSQL has no cross-database
    -- queries, so such an object confers nothing in this database and could not be resolved from
    -- this connection in any case; the claim this arm makes is bounded to this database exactly as
    -- the privilege-surface arm's residual (1) below already is.
    --
    -- THE REFUSAL NAMES CLASS + DATABASE + COUNT AND DELIBERATELY DOES NOT RESOLVE THE OBJECT.
    -- `pg_identify_object` does not error on a row belonging to another database — it returns a
    -- NULL identity — and a colliding OID could name the WRONG local object, so a message built
    -- from it would be worse than none. Class, database and count are always resolvable and always
    -- true. Note that `classid` is the dependency's own class and is not in general the catalog
    -- holding the owner column: a large object is keyed as `pg_largeobject`, not
    -- `pg_largeobject_metadata`, so this text must not be read as an owner-column catalogue.
    --
    -- THE THREE ARMS ABOVE STAY, BECAUSE THIS ONE DOES NOT SUBSUME THEM. Indexes, TOAST relations,
    -- relation rowtypes and array types are owned but record no `'o'` row of their own; they ride
    -- their principal, which does carry one. Rather than rely on that inference, this arm is
    -- strictly ADDITIVE: the three `EXISTS` arms are unchanged and remain the direct reading of
    -- the three classes that actually carry this unit's surface.
    --
    -- WHAT WAS MEASURED, AND WHAT IS COVERED BY CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN WITNESSED. A candidate
    -- owning only a database, only a schema, only a standalone type, only a large object, only a
    -- publication or only a tablespace produces exactly one `'o'` row that this scan sees. Five of
    -- those six — database, standalone type, large object, publication, tablespace — are invisible
    -- to the three arms above and are refused HERE. The sixth, an owned schema, is plain
    -- `pg_namespace.nspowner` and is caught by the arm above FIRST, so its refusal carries that
    -- arm's message, not this one's; that overlap is what makes the two complementary rather than
    -- one replacing the other.
    --
    -- EXTENSION OWNERSHIP IS NOW WITNESSED, AND THE REASON PREVIOUSLY GIVEN FOR NOT WITNESSING IT
    -- WAS WRONG. An earlier revision of this comment claimed a NOSUPERUSER candidate cannot be
    -- made to own an extension. It can: a TRUSTED extension requires only `CREATE` on the
    -- database, which is an ordinary grantable privilege and not a role attribute. Measured — a
    -- role carrying exactly this block's shape (NOLOGIN NOINHERIT NOBYPASSRLS NOSUPERUSER
    -- NOCREATEDB NOCREATEROLE NOREPLICATION) created and owned one after a temporary
    -- `GRANT CREATE ON DATABASE`, recording an `'o'` row under `pg_extension` while the
    -- three-catalog arm above still read ZERO. The evidence suite's extension-ownership control
    -- reproduces that plant, which is what converts this class from asserted to witnessed.
    -- Foreign-data-wrapper and subscription ownership remain covered BY CONSTRUCTION — the scan
    -- reads whatever PostgreSQL recorded, not a list — and are genuinely NOT witnessed: creating a
    -- foreign-data wrapper requires SUPERUSER outright, and creating a subscription requires
    -- membership in `pg_create_subscription`, which the zero-edges arm below independently forbids
    -- this candidate from holding.
    SELECT string_agg(t.line, '; ' ORDER BY t.line) INTO v_own_found
      FROM (SELECT s.classid::regclass::text || ' in '
                   || COALESCE((SELECT d.datname FROM pg_database d WHERE d.oid = s.dbid),
                               '<cluster-wide>')
                   || ' (' || count(*) || ')' AS line
              FROM pg_shdepend s
             WHERE s.refclassid = 'pg_authid'::regclass
               AND s.refobjid   = (SELECT oid FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = v_owner)
               AND s.deptype    = 'o'
               AND (s.dbid = 0
                    OR s.dbid = (SELECT d2.oid FROM pg_database d2
                                  WHERE d2.datname = current_database()))
             GROUP BY s.classid, s.dbid) t;
    IF v_own_found IS NOT NULL THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27: role % owns objects recorded as shared owner dependencies (%) — refusing to adopt a non-empty owner as the F-B operator owner',
        v_owner, v_own_found
        USING HINT = 'Ownership is authority a SECURITY DEFINER wrapper inherits whole, and it need not appear in any ACL. Drop or reassign those objects deliberately, or adopt a different role. This scan covers this database plus cluster-shared objects such as databases and tablespaces; the class shown is the dependency''s own class, not necessarily the catalog holding the owner column.';
    END IF;

    -- ── SERIALIZE THE AUDIENCE CHECK, BECAUSE A POINT-IN-TIME SCAN IS NOT A CLAIM ABOUT COMMIT.
    --
    -- The scan below reads a catalog snapshot. On the ADOPTION path the candidate is already
    -- globally visible, so any table owner in the cluster may commit
    -- `CREATE POLICY`/`ALTER POLICY … TO <candidate>` between that read and this transaction's
    -- COMMIT, and the install would then assert — silently, with no authorship at all — a property
    -- that is false the moment it is made. A second unlocked scan narrows that window; it cannot
    -- close it, because nothing orders the attacker's commit against ours.
    --
    -- WHAT WAS MEASURED, AND WHAT WAS RULED OUT BY MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Policy DDL
    -- holds no lasting `pg_policy` lock — it takes `RowExclusiveLock` transiently — so a post-hoc
    -- lock dump makes this look unfixable. What the transient lock does mean is that a
    -- `SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE` lock HELD by this transaction blocks every concurrent policy writer
    -- for the rest of it. Measured on PostgreSQL 18: `ACCESS SHARE`, `ROW SHARE`, `ROW EXCLUSIVE`
    -- and `SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE` do NOT block a concurrent `CREATE POLICY`; `SHARE`,
    -- `SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE`, `EXCLUSIVE` and `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` do. `SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE` is the
    -- weakest of those that also SELF-conflicts, so two concurrent installs queue instead of
    -- racing, while ordinary `ACCESS SHARE` catalog readers — every planner in the cluster — are
    -- left alone. Also ruled out by measurement: advisory locks (external DDL never consults
    -- them), `SERIALIZABLE`/`REPEATABLE READ` (catalogs are not predicate-locked), and locking the
    -- target table (that protects one table; the attack works on any other).
    --
    -- THE LOCK DOES NOT BLOCK THIS MIGRATION'S OWN POLICY WORK further down: same-transaction
    -- locks never conflict. And it is taken INSIDE this `DO` block deliberately — measured, a
    -- `LOCK TABLE` acquired here is held for the remainder of the transaction, including through
    -- the exception sub-block below, which is what lets the final re-verification prove it is
    -- still held rather than assume it.
    --
    -- WHY THE PRIVILEGE FAILURE IS CAUGHT AND NOTHING ELSE IS. `insufficient_privilege` is the ONE
    -- condition this lock raises that is about the deploying role rather than about concurrency,
    -- and it deserves a message naming the exact grant. Everything else propagates untouched — in
    -- particular `40P01`. Under concurrent policy DDL this transaction can be the deadlock victim,
    -- and that is the intended outcome: a killed migration installs NOTHING, which is fail-closed.
    -- There is deliberately no retry loop, no lock timeout and no `WHEN OTHERS`; a contended
    -- deploy aborts atomically and is retried by a human once the conflicting activity has ended.
    BEGIN
      LOCK TABLE pg_catalog.pg_policy IN SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE MODE;
    EXCEPTION WHEN insufficient_privilege THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27: this role may not lock pg_catalog.pg_policy, so role %''s policy audience cannot be checked under serialization — refusing to adopt an owner on an unserialized scan', v_owner
        USING ERRCODE = 'insufficient_privilege',
              HINT = 'Adoption serializes against concurrent policy DDL by locking pg_catalog.pg_policy above ACCESS SHARE. Measured on PostgreSQL 18, exactly three principals may take that lock: a superuser, a member of pg_maintain, or a role holding the MAINTAIN privilege on pg_catalog.pg_policy where that privilege was given inside THIS database. Confirm the deploying role carries one of the three before applying. This migration deliberately gives itself nothing and will not fall back to an unserialized check; the create path needs none of it.';
    END;
    -- NOTHING IS RECORDED HERE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. The final re-verification further down does
    -- not read a marker this branch wrote: it reads THE LOCK ITSELF, from `pg_locks`, filtered to
    -- rows this backend holds. A lock is a fact about this transaction that no configuration
    -- channel can produce and no other session can present on our behalf, whereas any setting —
    -- at any scope, transaction-local override included — is a read of the configuration stack
    -- that an ambient session-, role- or database-level value re-supplies the instant the
    -- override's transaction ends. That was measured, and it is why this branch's witness is the
    -- `SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE` lock taken immediately above rather than anything written down.

    -- ── AND THE LOCK ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH, SO AN IN-FLIGHT THIRD PARTY IS REFUSED OUTRIGHT.
    --
    -- MEASURED, AND THIS IS THE ARM THE LOCK CANNOT REPLACE. An attacker that has already RUN
    -- `CREATE POLICY … TO <candidate>` and is simply sitting on the open transaction has released
    -- the transient catalog lock, so this transaction acquires `SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE` happily; the
    -- scan below then cannot see the uncommitted row; both transactions commit; the foreign policy
    -- lands. Locking harder does not help — the writer is not holding the catalog.
    --
    -- WHAT IT IS STILL HOLDING IS THE AUDIENCE ITSELF. Measured: `CREATE POLICY … TO <role>` holds
    -- `AccessShareLock` on OBJECT `pg_authid:<that exact role>` until it commits, cluster-wide,
    -- and `pg_locks` shows it. So the second primitive is a probe keyed on the candidate's own
    -- OID. It is deliberately conservative in the fail-closed direction: the same probe also fires
    -- for a concurrent `ALTER POLICY … TO`, an ownership change and a grant aimed at this
    -- candidate, all of which are owner-targeting DDL this block would rather refuse than race.
    -- `granted` is NOT filtered, so a backend merely QUEUED on the candidate counts too.
    --
    -- IT REFUSES; IT DOES NOT WAIT. Waiting would re-introduce the same race one layer up (the
    -- other side may commit the moment the wait ends) and would make a migration's duration depend
    -- on a stranger's transaction. A client-side or advisory lock would be worse still AS A
    -- SERIALIZATION PRIMITIVE: external DDL does not consult one, so it would report safety it
    -- cannot deliver. (The create branch above holds no such key: the advisory witness it used to
    -- take is deleted, and so is the role-tuple witness that briefly replaced it. What tells the
    -- final re-verification which branch ran is the transaction-local sentinel that branch creates
    -- in `pg_temp`, which serializes nothing and claims nothing about third parties either.)
    --
    -- ── AND A PREPARED TRANSACTION HOLDS THAT AUDIENCE LOCK WITH NO BACKEND AT ALL ─────────────
    --
    -- THE PID CAN BE NULL, AND THE OBVIOUS PREDICATE THEN READS NOTHING. Measured on this server
    -- version: a session that runs `CREATE POLICY … TO <candidate>` and then `PREPARE TRANSACTION`
    -- disconnects entirely, yet its `pg_authid` object lock on the candidate REMAINS GRANTED with
    -- `pid` NULL. `l.pid <> pg_catalog.pg_backend_pid()` is NULL for such a row and therefore
    -- excludes it — the arm reads zero and admits the candidate — so the comparison below is
    -- `IS DISTINCT FROM`, which is true for a NULL pid.
    --
    -- AND THE AGGREGATE HAD THE SAME HOLE INDEPENDENTLY. `string_agg` skips NULL inputs, so even
    -- with the predicate fixed a NULL-pid row aggregates to NULL and the refusal still would not
    -- fire. Every holder is therefore rendered to a non-NULL identity before aggregation: an
    -- ordinary backend by its pid, a prepared holder by its global transaction id.
    --
    -- HOW A PREPARED HOLDER IS NAMED, AND WHY THE OBVIOUS JOIN IS DEAD. The documented shorthand —
    -- match `pg_locks.virtualtransaction` of the form `-1/<xid>` — does NOT hold here: measured,
    -- a prepared transaction's lock rows keep their ORIGINAL virtual transaction id, and no
    -- `-1/` row exists at all. What does exist is the prepared transaction's own `transactionid`
    -- lock row, carrying the same NULL pid and the same virtual transaction id, whose
    -- `transactionid` joins `pg_prepared_xacts` and yields the gid. That is the two-hop join
    -- below; if either hop is ever absent the holder still reports as its virtual transaction id,
    -- so the refusal cannot silently disappear.
    --
    -- AND THE LOCK ABOVE CANNOT COVER THIS ARM. Measured: `COMMIT PREPARED` proceeds while another
    -- backend holds `SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE` on `pg_catalog.pg_policy` — a prepared transaction's
    -- commit path does not re-acquire what it already holds — after which a fresh READ COMMITTED
    -- statement sees the row. So the serialization lock does NOT block this writer; the probe is
    -- the only thing that refuses it, which is exactly why it is fail-closed on a NULL pid.
    --
    -- THE MESSAGE NAMES THE ROLE AND THE HOLDERS AND NOTHING ELSE. A backend id — or a prepared
    -- transaction's gid — and this unit's own owner name are all the operator needs to find the
    -- conflicting session; the objects those holders are touching are not this migration's to
    -- disclose.
    SELECT string_agg(DISTINCT
             coalesce(l.pid::pg_catalog.text,
                      'prepared transaction ' || coalesce(px.gid, l.virtualtransaction)),
             ', ' ORDER BY
             coalesce(l.pid::pg_catalog.text,
                      'prepared transaction ' || coalesce(px.gid, l.virtualtransaction)))
      INTO v_pol_lock_pids
      FROM pg_catalog.pg_locks l
      LEFT JOIN pg_catalog.pg_locks t
             ON t.pid IS NULL
            AND t.locktype = 'transactionid'
            AND t.virtualtransaction = l.virtualtransaction
      LEFT JOIN pg_catalog.pg_prepared_xacts px
             ON px.transaction = t.transactionid
     WHERE l.locktype = 'object'
       AND l.classid  = 'pg_catalog.pg_authid'::pg_catalog.regclass
       AND l.objid    = (SELECT r.oid FROM pg_catalog.pg_roles r WHERE r.rolname = v_owner)
       AND l.pid IS DISTINCT FROM pg_catalog.pg_backend_pid();
    IF v_pol_lock_pids IS NOT NULL THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27: backend(s) % hold or await an owner-targeting lock on role % — refusing to adopt a candidate while somebody else''s uncommitted DDL is still aimed at it', v_pol_lock_pids, v_owner
        USING HINT = 'An uncommitted policy, ownership or grant statement naming this role is invisible to a catalog scan but still holds a pg_authid object lock on it, so adopting now would assert a property that the other transaction is about to falsify. A holder shown as a prepared transaction is a two-phase-commit participant with no backend of its own — find it in pg_prepared_xacts by that gid and COMMIT PREPARED or ROLLBACK PREPARED it deliberately. Otherwise let that session commit or roll back, inspect what it did, and re-run this migration. Nothing has been written.';
    END IF;

    -- AN ADOPTED OWNER MUST NOT ALREADY BE ADMITTED BY A POLICY SOMEBODY ELSE WROTE. The arms
    -- above bound what the candidate IS and what it OWNS; the arm below bounds what has been
    -- GRANTED TO it. None of the three can see a policy that NAMES it. A permissive policy
    -- `TO <candidate>` is neither a privilege nor an ownership — it is row VISIBILITY, recorded
    -- only as an `'r'` shared dependency — and it converts a table the candidate can already
    -- reach (through PUBLIC, or through any privilege this migration itself grants below) from
    -- zero readable rows into real ones. Every SECURITY DEFINER wrapper this unit installs then
    -- inherits that visibility whole, which is the exact property §0b exists to bound.
    --
    -- THE SCOPE IS THIS DATABASE PLUS SHARED OBJECTS, AND HERE THAT BOUND IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER
    -- THAN MERELY CONSISTENT. Measured: a policy `TO <role>` records exactly ONE row, `classid` of
    -- `pg_policy`, `dbid` = the CURRENT database and NEVER `0`; a `TO PUBLIC` policy records no
    -- role row at all, because PUBLIC is OID 0 and is not a role. THIS FILE ITSELF CREATES SUCH A
    -- POLICY ON ITS OWN OWNER further down — `D7 owner can verify caller manager membership` on
    -- `public.academy_managers`. An unscoped scan would therefore read database one's row while
    -- installing into a SECOND database of the same cluster — the evidence suite's own topology —
    -- and refuse an install whose only offence is that the first install succeeded. So this arm
    -- carries the ownership arm's exact `dbid = 0 OR this database` bound, for that reason.
    --
    -- AND THE BOUND IS DISCLOSED HONESTLY. A policy in ANOTHER database of this cluster is NOT
    -- refused here, and it confers nothing here: PostgreSQL has no cross-database queries, a
    -- policy gates rows of a table in the database that holds it, and no connection from this
    -- database could read those rows in any case. That is a true statement about reach rather than
    -- a gap — and it is the exact opposite of database OWNERSHIP, which is recorded `dbid = 0`,
    -- is cluster-visible, and IS refused by the arm above.
    SELECT string_agg(t.line, '; ' ORDER BY t.line) INTO v_pol_found
      FROM (SELECT s.classid::regclass::text || ' in '
                   || COALESCE((SELECT d.datname FROM pg_database d WHERE d.oid = s.dbid),
                               '<cluster-wide>')
                   || ' (' || count(*) || ')' AS line
              FROM pg_shdepend s
             WHERE s.refclassid = 'pg_authid'::regclass
               AND s.refobjid   = (SELECT oid FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = v_owner)
               AND s.deptype    = 'r'
               AND (s.dbid = 0
                    OR s.dbid = (SELECT d2.oid FROM pg_database d2
                                  WHERE d2.datname = current_database()))
             GROUP BY s.classid, s.dbid) t;
    IF v_pol_found IS NOT NULL THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27: role % is already named as an RLS policy audience (%) — refusing to adopt a candidate that pre-existing policies already admit',
        v_owner, v_pol_found
        USING HINT = 'A policy audience is row visibility a SECURITY DEFINER wrapper inherits whole, and it is neither a privilege nor an ownership, so no other adoption arm can see it. Re-aim or drop those policies deliberately, or adopt a different role. This scan covers this database plus cluster-shared objects; a policy in another database of this cluster is not refused and confers no visibility here.';
    END IF;

    -- AN ADOPTED OWNER MUST ALSO ARRIVE WITH AN EMPTY PRIVILEGE SURFACE. The arms above bound what
    -- the candidate IS (attributes) and what it OWNS, and the arm below bounds who it is JOINED
    -- to. None of the three looks at what has already been GRANTED TO it. A role can own no object
    -- and carry no membership edge and still hold `SELECT` on `auth.users`, `EXECUTE` on somebody
    -- else's definer routine, or a default privilege that will hand it every future table in a
    -- schema. Adopting one would make this section's own claim — "the set of privileges granted TO
    -- THIS ROLE below and nothing else granted to it" — false at install time, because a SECURITY
    -- DEFINER wrapper runs with every privilege its owner holds, not merely with the ones
    -- enumerated below.
    --
    -- THE INVENTORY IS DERIVED FROM THE SERVER, NOT ASSUMED. Hand-listing object classes is how
    -- this arm would rot: a class this migration did not think of, or one a later PostgreSQL adds,
    -- would go on granting to the adopted owner from outside the check. The scan therefore asks
    -- the running server for every `aclitem[]` column in its own catalog and covers all of them.
    -- On PostgreSQL 18 that is thirteen: `pg_attribute`, `pg_class`, `pg_database`,
    -- `pg_default_acl`, `pg_foreign_data_wrapper`, `pg_foreign_server`, `pg_language`,
    -- `pg_largeobject_metadata`, `pg_namespace`, `pg_parameter_acl`, `pg_proc`, `pg_tablespace`
    -- and `pg_type`. `pg_attribute` is not optional: a column-level grant leaves `pg_class.relacl`
    -- NULL outright, so a relation-only scan sees nothing at all. `pg_default_acl` is not optional
    -- either — it grants on objects that do not exist yet, so it is invisible to every scan of
    -- present-tense object ACLs.
    --
    -- WHY RAW ACL EXPANSION AND NOT `has_*_privilege`. That family answers "could this role do
    -- it", which is equally true when the role OWNS the object, when the privilege sits on PUBLIC,
    -- and when it arrives through a membership — so it cannot serve as positive authority about a
    -- grant made TO this candidate. `aclexplode` keyed to the candidate's OID names the grantee
    -- exactly, and carries `is_grantable`, so a grant-option right refuses on the same footing as
    -- the privilege it can pass on.
    --
    -- WHAT IS EXCLUDED, DELIBERATELY. `grantor = grantee = candidate` is the shape PostgreSQL
    -- materializes for an object's OWN owner — and for the creator half of a default ACL — the
    -- moment any grant forces the ACL to be written out. That is the implicit owner right, not an
    -- acquired one; the ownership arm above is what governs it, and counting it here would make
    -- this arm fire on the case that one already refuses. `pg_init_privs` is excluded because it
    -- is pg_dump's record of privileges as they stood at extension or initdb time rather than a
    -- live grant: the live grant is in the object's own ACL column, which IS scanned, while a
    -- since-revoked entry lingering in `initprivs` would refuse a privilege nobody holds.
    --
    -- THE RESIDUAL IS DISCLOSED, NOT CONCEALED, and it has three named parts. (1) Ten of the
    -- thirteen catalogs are per-database, so what this proves is the candidate's privilege surface
    -- in THIS database plus the three cluster-wide ones (`pg_database`, `pg_parameter_acl`,
    -- `pg_tablespace`); a grant made in a different database of the same cluster is not visible
    -- from this connection and cannot be made so from inside a migration. (2) Privileges held by
    -- PUBLIC are deliberately NOT counted: they are ambient facts of the database that every role
    -- carries identically, so they are the same whether this block mints a fresh owner or adopts
    -- one — refusing on them would be refusing on the database's state rather than the candidate's,
    -- and no choice of candidate could satisfy it. (3) The scan keys on the GRANTEE, so a default
    -- ACL in the other direction — one whose `defaclrole` IS the candidate, arming objects the
    -- candidate later creates — is not this arm's business: it grants to others, not to the owner.
    -- It is not unguarded, though; §0c's 2f pins the whole `pg_default_acl` inventory to its three
    -- predecessor seeds and refuses any fourth row. All three are stated bounds on the claim, not
    -- holes in the scan, and the refusal always names the catalog and privilege it actually found.
    SELECT r.oid INTO v_acl_oid FROM pg_roles r WHERE r.rolname = v_owner;
    FOR v_acl_cat, v_acl_col IN
      SELECT c.relname, a.attname
        FROM pg_attribute a
        JOIN pg_class c ON c.oid = a.attrelid
        JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = c.relnamespace
       WHERE n.nspname = 'pg_catalog'
         AND c.relkind = 'r'
         AND a.atttypid = 'pg_catalog._aclitem'::regtype
         AND a.attnum > 0
         AND c.relname <> 'pg_init_privs'
       ORDER BY c.relname, a.attname
    LOOP
      EXECUTE format(
        'SELECT array_agg(DISTINCT x.privilege_type
                            || CASE WHEN x.is_grantable THEN '' WITH GRANT OPTION'' ELSE '''' END)
           FROM pg_catalog.%I t, LATERAL pg_catalog.aclexplode(t.%I) x
          WHERE x.grantee = $1 AND x.grantor <> $1',
        v_acl_cat, v_acl_col)
        INTO v_acl_privs USING v_acl_oid;
      IF v_acl_privs IS NOT NULL THEN
        v_acl_found := v_acl_found
          || format('%s.%s: %s', v_acl_cat, v_acl_col, array_to_string(v_acl_privs, ', '));
      END IF;
    END LOOP;
    IF cardinality(v_acl_found) > 0 THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27: role % already holds privileges granted to it (%) — refusing to adopt a role with a pre-existing privilege surface as the F-B operator owner',
        v_owner, array_to_string(v_acl_found, '; ')
        USING HINT = 'Domain A''s blast radius is the set of privileges this migration grants TO THIS ROLE, and nothing else granted to it. That bound is on grants made to the role: privileges held by PUBLIC, and the defaults a NULL ACL column implies, remain effective for this role exactly as they do for every other one, and are identical whether this block mints a fresh owner or adopts an existing one. Revoke the pre-existing grants and default privileges deliberately, or adopt a different role; this migration will not install over a privilege surface it did not review.';
    END IF;
  END IF;

  -- ZERO MEMBERSHIP EDGES, IN EITHER DIRECTION, WITH ANY ROLE AT ALL.
  --
  -- The receipt's owner shape is `NO_MEMBERSHIPS` — not merely "no path to the three runtime
  -- roots". The earlier revision proved runtime non-reachability recursively; Stage 7.4-A
  -- strengthens it to the exact receipt shape, which SUBSUMES every transitive path: a role with
  -- no membership edge is on no chain. (A pre-existing exact-attribute role granted to some
  -- fourth login role passed the old check and handed that login `SET ROLE` into the owner —
  -- and, through it, every owner-private core plus the then-retained direct cycles grant, which
  -- Stage 7.4-C has since revoked.) §10c reads the membership topology again after install, and
  -- the final re-verification reads it once more under the product locks — but neither can PREVENT
  -- a membership grant that lands after the last read, because role membership lives in a shared
  -- catalog no lock this file may take excludes. What bounds that surface is the approved
  -- installation window, not these scans; the final block states the same thing where it re-reads
  -- them.
  IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_auth_members m
              WHERE m.roleid = (SELECT oid FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = v_owner)
                 OR m.member = (SELECT oid FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = v_owner)) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27: role % carries a role-membership edge — the F-B operator owner must have none, in either direction, with any role', v_owner
      USING HINT = 'The receipt pins NOLOGIN/NOINHERIT/NOBYPASSRLS/NOSUPERUSER with NO memberships. Remove the edge deliberately; this migration will not adopt over it.';
  END IF;

  -- ── EXACT DEPENDENCY PRIVILEGES, AND NOTHING ELSE ────────────────────────────────────────
  --
  -- A SECURITY DEFINER function runs with the owner's privileges, so every object its body
  -- touches must be reachable BY THE OWNER. These are enumerated deliberately rather than
  -- granted wholesale: the owner needs to resolve `public`, call `auth.uid()`, and read/write
  -- exactly the round-authority tables its cores operate on.
  EXECUTE format('GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO %I', v_owner);

  -- `auth` USAGE + EXECUTE on auth.uid() ONLY — meaning no OTHER privilege is GRANTED TO the owner
  -- on `auth.users` or any other auth object: the wrappers need the caller's subject, never the
  -- auth tables themselves. As above, the bound is on grants made to this role; anything `auth`
  -- exposes to PUBLIC, or by a NULL-ACL default, reaches this role as it reaches every other, and
  -- is the same before and after this block runs.
  IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_namespace WHERE nspname = 'auth') THEN
    EXECUTE format('GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA auth TO %I', v_owner);
    IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_proc p JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = p.pronamespace
                WHERE n.nspname = 'auth' AND p.proname = 'uid' AND p.pronargs = 0) THEN
      EXECUTE format('GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION auth.uid() TO %I', v_owner);
    ELSE
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27: auth.uid() is required — the F-B operator wrappers derive every actor from it and accept no actor argument';
    END IF;
  ELSE
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27: schema auth is required for the F-B operator topology';
  END IF;
END $abc27_owner$;

-- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- §0c. ABORT-FIRST DOMAIN-P DISCOVERY (receipt 87c0cd41…).
-- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- WHY A THIRD DOMAIN. Domain A is NOLOGIN/NOINHERIT/NOBYPASSRLS and does not own the product
-- tables. A table GRANT does not bypass RLS, so granting A rights on `cycles` et al is both too
-- broad as a design and still insufficient under RLS — the combination that made the previous
-- attempt unexecutable. The approved answer is Domain P: the EXISTING owner of exactly nine
-- product relations, reached only through closed purpose-specific P-owned helpers.
--
-- WHY ABORT-FIRST, AND WHY BEFORE EVERY D7 OBJECT. This block runs before the first D7 relation
-- is created and before the `academy_managers` policy composition below, so a drifted product
-- catalog stops the migration while the database is still untouched by this unit. Discovery is
-- NOT permission to accept whatever the catalog happens to hold: the expected owner is the exact
-- literal `postgres`, and anything else stops for a second owner decision rather than adapting.
--
-- WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE HERE. No ownership transfer, no role creation or ALTER, no
-- "same attributes means same owner" fallback, no A table/column grant, no policy widening and
-- no BYPASSRLS. Each of those was considered and rejected in the bound proposal.
--
-- THE RESIDUAL IS DISCLOSED, NOT CONCEALED. `postgres` may legitimately be SUPERUSER, BYPASSRLS
-- and login-capable. Helper ACLs cannot constrain an object owner, and a superuser bypasses ACLs
-- outright. This block therefore proves the thing that IS provable — that no runtime role and no
-- membership chain can BECOME either owner — asserts the exact pinned attribute vector (Stage
-- 7.4-A: this migration's only authorized execution context is the pinned local predecessor, so
-- the vector is a fact to hold, not a discovery to log), and still raises it as a NOTICE so the
-- install log carries the honest residual instead of an implied containment claim.
DO $abc27_domain_p_stage0$
DECLARE
  v_a          CONSTANT name := 'padeltrainer_abc27_owner';
  v_expected_p CONSTANT name := 'postgres';
  v_tables     CONSTANT text[] := ARRAY[
    'academy_profiles','cycles','availability_slots','bookings','slot_priority_claims',
    'trainer_profiles','profiles','guest_players','locations'
  ];
  v_obj        text;
  v_owner      name;
  v_n_owner    name;
  v_super      boolean;
  v_bypass     boolean;
  v_login      boolean;
  v_inherit    boolean;
  v_createrole boolean;
  v_createdb   boolean;
  v_repl       boolean;
  -- Stage 7.4-A: the pinned policy-, column-, constraint- and index-inventory digests computed
  -- against the live catalog.
  --
  -- …and the audience states that would make the policy digest below non-injective, collected
  -- before it is computed rather than discovered by a digest that silently agrees.
  v_audience_defect   text;
  v_policy_digest     text;
  v_column_digest     text;
  v_constraint_digest text;
  v_index_digest      text;
  -- Stage 7.4-A round 7: the claim-token generator execution-chain digest (2c-c).
  v_generator_chain_digest text;
  v_wrapper_body           text;
  -- Round 8: the `extensions` namespace authority digest and the exact resolution candidate
  -- sets for the generator name in both schemas it lives in.
  v_extensions_ns_digest   text;
  v_generator_candidates   text[];
BEGIN
  -- (1) THE EXACT NINE RELATIONS EXIST AND CARRY THE EXACT EXPECTED OWNER.
  --
  -- The set is closed and written out literally. It is never derived from the migration's own
  -- source at runtime, because a set inferred from code would silently grow the moment a helper
  -- referenced a tenth table — exactly the drift this gate exists to refuse.
  FOREACH v_obj IN ARRAY v_tables LOOP
    IF to_regclass('public.' || quote_ident(v_obj)) IS NULL THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: required Domain-P relation public.% does not exist', v_obj
        USING HINT = 'The approved product-table set is closed at nine relations. A missing member stops for a second owner decision; it is not created, substituted or inferred here.';
    END IF;

    SELECT c.relowner::regrole::name
      INTO v_owner
      FROM pg_class c JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = c.relnamespace
     WHERE n.nspname = 'public' AND c.relname = v_obj;

    IF v_owner IS DISTINCT FROM v_expected_p THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: public.% is owned by %, not the expected Domain-P owner % — refusing to continue', v_obj, v_owner, v_expected_p
        USING HINT = 'Do not transfer ownership, create/alter a role, or fall back to the migration executor. A different or split product owner is a second owner decision.';
    END IF;

  END LOOP;

  -- (2) THE PINNED LOCAL PREDECESSOR BASELINE — CLOSED ROWS, COUNTS AND DIGESTS (Stage 7.4-A).
  --
  -- The earlier revision only NOTICE-logged this inventory, on the argument that the local
  -- fixture and the hosted project differ. Stage 7.4-A inverts that: this migration's only
  -- authorized execution context is the disposable local predecessor built from the pinned
  -- fixture and lineage, so the baseline it was proved against IS knowable, and anything else is
  -- drift to refuse — not an environment to adapt to. A future production application is a
  -- separately gated event whose own preflight must re-derive these facts against production
  -- state and re-rule this block; installing there today is SUPPOSED to stop.
  --
  -- Every assertion below is a closed expected set compared in BOTH directions — a missing row
  -- and an extra row refuse alike — never a live-derived value compared to itself.
  --
  -- 2pre. PLAIN HEAP RELATIONS, WITH NO INHERITANCE EDGE IN EITHER DIRECTION (Stage 7.4-A
  --       review). Every product query in this unit deliberately writes `FROM x`, not
  --       `FROM ONLY x` — the shipped predecessor shape is nine ordinary tables, so the forms are
  --       equivalent there. That equivalence is exactly what an INHERITS child or a partition
  --       bolted onto one of the nine would break: its rows would enter eligibility, the freeze
  --       capture and the sibling counts while its owner, ACL, policies, constraints and
  --       triggers sit entirely outside this proof (and a `cycles` descendant would not even
  --       carry the row guards, which do not inherit). So the gate refuses any relkind other
  --       than an ordinary table, any partition membership, and any `pg_inherits` edge touching
  --       the nine — in either direction.
  IF EXISTS (
    SELECT 1
      FROM unnest(v_tables) AS t(name)
      JOIN pg_class c ON c.oid = to_regclass('public.' || t.name)
     WHERE c.relkind <> 'r'
        OR c.relispartition
        OR EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_inherits i WHERE i.inhparent = c.oid OR i.inhrelid = c.oid)
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: a Domain-P relation is not a plain heap table or carries an inheritance/partition edge — descendant relations would enter product queries outside this proof'
      USING HINT = 'The approved set is nine ordinary tables. A partitioned or inherited shape is a different product design and stops for a second owner decision.';
  END IF;

  -- 2a. RLS/FORCE FLAGS, exact per relation. EVERY ONE OF THE NINE ships with RLS enabled in the
  --     production lineage, and FORCE is off on all nine. Both halves are load-bearing and they
  --     say opposite things: RLS-on is why direct client DML against these relations is contained
  --     at all, while FORCE-off is why the P-owned helpers — which execute as the table owner —
  --     are NOT contained by it. That is the authority shape this checkpoint was reviewed
  --     against, and it is precisely why this unit's capabilities and guards, rather than RLS,
  --     are what bound the P writers.
  IF EXISTS (
    SELECT 1
      FROM (VALUES
        ('academy_profiles',     true), ('availability_slots',   true), ('bookings',             true),
        ('cycles',               true), ('guest_players',        true), ('locations',            true),
        ('profiles',             true), ('slot_priority_claims', true), ('trainer_profiles',     true)
      ) AS expected(relname, rls)
      JOIN pg_class c ON c.oid = to_regclass('public.' || expected.relname)
     WHERE c.relrowsecurity IS DISTINCT FROM expected.rls
        OR c.relforcerowsecurity IS DISTINCT FROM false
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: a Domain-P relation''s RLS or FORCE flag drifted from the pinned predecessor baseline'
      USING HINT = 'Expected RLS enabled on ALL NINE relations, FORCE off on all nine. A different shape means this is not the reviewed predecessor; stop rather than adapt.';
  END IF;

  -- 2b. RELATION ACLs. The predecessor seeds every one of the nine identically: the owner plus
  --     the three runtime roles, each holding all eight table privileges, none grantable, nobody
  --     else. That blanket seed is exactly why RLS (where enabled) and this unit's own guards are
  --     load-bearing, so it is pinned rather than assumed.
  IF EXISTS (
    SELECT 1
      FROM unnest(v_tables) AS t(name)
      JOIN pg_class c ON c.oid = to_regclass('public.' || t.name)
      CROSS JOIN LATERAL aclexplode(coalesce(c.relacl, acldefault('r', c.relowner))) a
     WHERE a.is_grantable
        OR a.grantee NOT IN (SELECT r.oid FROM pg_roles r
                              WHERE r.rolname IN ('postgres','anon','authenticated','service_role'))
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: a Domain-P relation carries an ACL entry outside the pinned predecessor seed (owner + three runtime roles, non-grantable)';
  END IF;
  IF EXISTS (
    SELECT 1
      FROM unnest(v_tables) AS t(name)
      JOIN pg_class c ON c.oid = to_regclass('public.' || t.name)
      CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
        SELECT count(*) AS n FROM aclexplode(coalesce(c.relacl, acldefault('r', c.relowner)))
      ) x
     WHERE x.n <> 32
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: a Domain-P relation''s ACL cardinality drifted from the pinned 4-role x 8-privilege predecessor seed';
  END IF;

  -- 2c. ZERO COLUMN ACLs anywhere on the nine. A column grant is a narrower authority the
  --     reviewed baseline does not contain; one appearing means a different security design.
  IF EXISTS (
    SELECT 1
      FROM unnest(v_tables) AS t(name)
      JOIN pg_attribute att ON att.attrelid = to_regclass('public.' || t.name)
     WHERE att.attnum > 0 AND NOT att.attisdropped AND att.attacl IS NOT NULL
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: a Domain-P relation carries a column-level ACL, which the pinned predecessor baseline does not contain';
  END IF;

  -- 2c-b. THE COMPLETE COLUMN INVENTORY OF THE NINE, AS ONE DIGEST (review round 5). The check
  --       above closes column ACLs; nothing before this round closed the COLUMNS themselves. A
  --       predecessor-added `GENERATED ALWAYS AS (fn(...)) STORED` column is unmanifested
  --       executable code on the product write path: the retained apply status update and the P
  --       lifecycle update would execute it AFTER acquiring product locks, and it can read
  --       relations and take lock edges no capability names — while no ACL, policy, trigger,
  --       rule, constraint or index digest changes at all. The same hole admits a plain added
  --       column (a write surface outside the reviewed shape), a dropped or reordered column
  --       (every reviewed statement now binds different attributes), a rewritten DEFAULT
  --       (unreviewed code in every INSERT that omits the column), and single-fact shape drift
  --       (nullability, type/typmod, collation, identity/generated state, storage/compression,
  --       locality/inheritance). So the complete per-relation attribute vector of every
  --       non-dropped user column is frozen as one digest, in deterministic (relation, attnum)
  --       order: identity and order, rendered type plus raw typmod and array dimensionality,
  --       collation, nullability, identity/generated markers, default presence, storage and
  --       compression, locality/inheritance facts, the fast-default marker, and the exact
  --       `pg_attrdef` DEFAULT/GENERATED expression as `pg_get_expr` renders it. The
  --       fast-default STORED CONSTANT (`attmissingval`) is deliberately NOT serialized: for a
  --       volatile default it is the evaluated install-time value — two byte-identical
  --       predecessor builds disagree on it by construction — so the pinnable facts are the
  --       marker and the expression, and both are pinned.
  --
  --       EXECUTABLE DEPENDENCIES ARE CLOSED WITH THE EXPRESSION, not by OID: `CREATE OR
  --       REPLACE` preserves both a routine's OID and every expression text that references it,
  --       so an unchanged deparse can hide a swapped body. Every `pg_depend` edge from a column
  --       default to a routine — and to an operator, whose underlying function is the same
  --       replace-in-place surface — is therefore serialized by stable schema/name/identity plus
  --       the security-relevant definition facts (owner, security mode, volatility, language,
  --       configuration, source digest), the same vector 2e-c pins for the trigger functions.
  --       Pinned builtins record no dependency edge and cannot be replaced in place. The pinned
  --       predecessor records exactly ONE edge — `slot_priority_claims.claim_token`'s default
  --       calls the public `gen_random_bytes(integer)` SQL wrapper (pgcrypto itself lives in
  --       schema `extensions` behind it) — and that edge is precisely the load-bearing case: a
  --       superuser CREATE OR REPLACE can swap that function's body under the unchanged
  --       default text, and only these dependency facts see it. Any OTHER edge appearing
  --       refuses as digest drift, and a new edge cannot attach without an expression change
  --       this digest already refuses. What these facts alone still cannot see is the chain
  --       BEHIND the wrapper's string body — 2c-c below closes exactly that.
  --
  --       Separators nest without collision: \x1e between attribute records, \x1f between
  --       fields, \x1d between dependency entries inside the dependency field, \x1c between the
  --       facts of one dependency entry. The pin is the pristine-predecessor value under exactly
  --       this serialization (captured from the shipped computation itself, like 2g-d). The §0
  --       preflight keeps its dedicated `slot_priority_claims.status` shape refusal as the
  --       legible first diagnostic; this digest is the closure over all nine relations.
  SELECT encode(pg_catalog.sha256(pg_catalog.convert_to(coalesce(string_agg(
           c.relname
           || E'\x1f' || a.attname
           || E'\x1f' || a.attnum::text
           || E'\x1f' || format_type(a.atttypid, a.atttypmod)
           || E'\x1f' || a.atttypmod::text
           || E'\x1f' || a.attndims::text
           || E'\x1f' || coalesce((SELECT cn.nspname || '.' || co.collname
                                     FROM pg_collation co
                                     JOIN pg_namespace cn ON cn.oid = co.collnamespace
                                    WHERE co.oid = a.attcollation), '<none>')
           || E'\x1f' || a.attnotnull::text
           || E'\x1f' || coalesce(nullif(a.attidentity::text, ''), '<none>')
           || E'\x1f' || coalesce(nullif(a.attgenerated::text, ''), '<none>')
           || E'\x1f' || a.atthasdef::text
           || E'\x1f' || a.attstorage::text
           || E'\x1f' || coalesce(nullif(a.attcompression::text, ''), '<default>')
           || E'\x1f' || a.attislocal::text
           || E'\x1f' || a.attinhcount::text
           || E'\x1f' || a.atthasmissing::text
           || E'\x1f' || coalesce(pg_get_expr(d.adbin, d.adrelid), '<none>')
           || E'\x1f' || coalesce(dep.entries, '<none>'),
           E'\x1e' ORDER BY c.relname, a.attnum), ''), 'UTF8')), 'hex')
    INTO v_column_digest
    FROM pg_attribute a
    JOIN pg_class c ON c.oid = a.attrelid
    JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = c.relnamespace
    LEFT JOIN pg_attrdef d ON d.adrelid = a.attrelid AND d.adnum = a.attnum
    LEFT JOIN LATERAL (
      SELECT string_agg(e.entry, E'\x1d' ORDER BY e.entry) AS entries
        FROM (
          SELECT pn.nspname || '.' || pr.proname
                 || '(' || pg_get_function_identity_arguments(pr.oid) || ')'
                 || E'\x1c' || pr.proowner::regrole::text
                 || E'\x1c' || pr.prosecdef::text
                 || E'\x1c' || pr.provolatile::text
                 || E'\x1c' || pl.lanname
                 || E'\x1c' || coalesce(pr.proconfig::text, '<none>')
                 || E'\x1c' || encode(pg_catalog.sha256(pg_catalog.convert_to(coalesce(pr.prosrc, ''), 'UTF8')), 'hex')
                 -- Round 8: the planner-support routine is part of the executable surface — a
                 -- support hook can rewrite the call at plan time under an unchanged body.
                 || E'\x1c' || pr.prosupport::regproc::text AS entry
            FROM pg_depend dp
            JOIN pg_proc pr ON pr.oid = dp.refobjid
            JOIN pg_language pl ON pl.oid = pr.prolang
            JOIN pg_namespace pn ON pn.oid = pr.pronamespace
           WHERE dp.classid = 'pg_attrdef'::regclass
             AND dp.objid = d.oid
             AND dp.refclassid = 'pg_proc'::regclass
          UNION ALL
          SELECT opn.nspname || '.' || op.oprname
                 || '(' || coalesce(format_type(nullif(op.oprleft, 0), NULL), '<none>')
                 || ',' || coalesce(format_type(nullif(op.oprright, 0), NULL), '<none>') || ')'
                 || E'\x1c' || fpn.nspname || '.' || fpr.proname
                 || '(' || pg_get_function_identity_arguments(fpr.oid) || ')'
                 || E'\x1c' || fpr.proowner::regrole::text
                 || E'\x1c' || fpr.prosecdef::text
                 || E'\x1c' || fpr.provolatile::text
                 || E'\x1c' || fpl.lanname
                 || E'\x1c' || coalesce(fpr.proconfig::text, '<none>')
                 || E'\x1c' || encode(pg_catalog.sha256(pg_catalog.convert_to(coalesce(fpr.prosrc, ''), 'UTF8')), 'hex')
                 || E'\x1c' || fpr.prosupport::regproc::text AS entry
            FROM pg_depend dp
            JOIN pg_operator op ON op.oid = dp.refobjid
            JOIN pg_namespace opn ON opn.oid = op.oprnamespace
            JOIN pg_proc fpr ON fpr.oid = op.oprcode
            JOIN pg_language fpl ON fpl.oid = fpr.prolang
            JOIN pg_namespace fpn ON fpn.oid = fpr.pronamespace
           WHERE dp.classid = 'pg_attrdef'::regclass
             AND dp.objid = d.oid
             AND dp.refclassid = 'pg_operator'::regclass
        ) e
    ) dep ON true
   WHERE n.nspname = 'public' AND c.relname = ANY (v_tables)
     AND a.attnum > 0 AND NOT a.attisdropped;
  -- STAGE 7.4-C — DERIVED FROM THE REAL LINEAGE, NOT FROM A FIXTURE. Every earlier value of this
  -- pin was measured against a predecessor whose nine relations were hand-written stubs: the
  -- suite created a minimal `availability_slots`, `bookings`, `cycles` and so on, and then
  -- extended them column by column as each reviewed contract needed more. A digest taken over
  -- that surface freezes the FIXTURE's shape, so it could neither refuse nor even see a change
  -- to the columns production actually has. This value comes from one clean disposable install
  -- of the full repaired 614-file lineage plus `20261118115500` — no stub, no fixup, no
  -- hand-written DDL anywhere on the proof path — using the serialization immediately above and
  -- its deterministic (relation, attnum) ordering. Its discriminating control is an added column
  -- on any of the nine, which moves it and nothing else.
  IF v_column_digest IS DISTINCT FROM '23631c2c067c873bcb667c0e0f56be0140f576db5ba366887da9ba38911429a4' THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: the Domain-P column inventory digest is %, not the pinned predecessor baseline', v_column_digest
      USING HINT = 'A column was added, removed, reordered or reshaped, a DEFAULT or GENERATED expression changed, or a routine behind one changed, on one of the nine relations. Column expressions execute inside every product write, so an unreviewed one is unreviewed code on the product path; stop rather than adapt.';
  END IF;

  -- 2c-c. THE CLAIM-TOKEN GENERATOR EXECUTION CHAIN, CLOSED END TO END (round 7). 2c-b's
  --       dependency facts bind the ONE recorded `pg_attrdef` edge — the public
  --       `gen_random_bytes(integer)` wrapper — but that wrapper is a string-bodied SQL shim
  --       calling `extensions.gen_random_bytes(integer)`, and PostgreSQL records NO catalog
  --       dependency edge for a call inside a string body: the inner implementation could be
  --       replaced in place (deterministic output — enumerable, predictable bearer tokens
  --       reachable through the anonymous claim RPCs) while every 2c-b fact stayed identical.
  --       So the exact shipped default's COMPLETE execution chain is bound here, by stable
  --       schema-qualified name — never by OID, and never through a transitive `pg_depend`
  --       edge PostgreSQL does not record for a string-bodied SQL wrapper:
  --
  --         1. public.gen_random_bytes(integer)      — the wrapper the default names;
  --         2. extensions.gen_random_bytes(integer)  — the pgcrypto implementation it calls.
  --
  --       For each routine the digest serializes (round 9) the COMPLETE CALLABLE AND RESULT
  --       SHAPE: identity, owner, prokind, and the full catalog definition rendered by
  --       pg_get_functiondef — which carries the result type and SETOF-ness, every argument
  --       mode/name/default, variadic form, language, volatility, strictness, leakproofness,
  --       parallel safety, definer mode, configuration, COST/ROWS and the body or 'obj','sym'
  --       binding verbatim — plus the two catalog facts functiondef does not reliably render:
  --       protrftypes (TRANSFORM bindings) and the planner-support routine (round 8: required
  --       to be zero by the explicit arm below — a support hook rewrites calls at plan time
  --       under an unchanged body); then extension membership, the RAW ACL (every aclitem —
  --       grantee, privilege, grantability — with the built-in default rendered when proacl
  --       is NULL) and the EFFECTIVE EXECUTE vector for the exact reviewed role classes that
  --       can exercise the product default: postgres, anon, authenticated, service_role. A
  --       missing routine serializes as '<absent>' and refuses; a missing OR added raw entry
  --       or effective path refuses identically. No EXECUTE is granted or revoked here: the
  --       pin freezes the reviewed authority, it does not adjust it.
  --
  --       The wrapper-to-inner CALL SHAPE is additionally pinned as the exact body text below,
  --       so a rewrite of the shim cannot retarget the chain while the serialized facts keep
  --       their shape. 2c-b still refuses wrapper drift first (it runs earlier); this block
  --       extends the refusal through the transitive implementation and its authority.
  SELECT p.prosrc INTO v_wrapper_body
    FROM pg_proc p WHERE p.oid = to_regprocedure('public.gen_random_bytes(integer)');
  IF v_wrapper_body IS DISTINCT FROM E'\n  SELECT extensions.gen_random_bytes(len);\n' THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: the claim-token generator wrapper body is not the exact reviewed shim (found %)',
      coalesce(quote_literal(v_wrapper_body), '<absent>')
      USING HINT = 'public.gen_random_bytes(integer) must be exactly the one-statement SELECT of extensions.gen_random_bytes(len). Any other call shape re-routes every freshly minted claim_token; stop rather than adapt.';
  END IF;

  -- ROUND 8: NO PLANNER-SUPPORT HOOK ON EITHER LINK. `prosupport` names a routine the PLANNER
  -- executes for calls of the function, and a support hook answering SupportRequestSimplify can
  -- replace the generator expression at plan time while body, OID, owner, language, source,
  -- binary, volatility, ACL and extension membership all read exactly as pinned. The chain is
  -- reviewed with ZERO support routines; the digest below also serializes the field, and this
  -- explicit arm keeps the refusal legible.
  IF EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM pg_proc p
     WHERE p.oid IN (to_regprocedure('public.gen_random_bytes(integer)'),
                     to_regprocedure('extensions.gen_random_bytes(integer)'))
       AND p.prosupport <> 0
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: a claim-token generator routine carries a planner-support function — the executing chain is no longer the reviewed two-routine chain'
      USING HINT = 'prosupport must be 0 for both public.gen_random_bytes(integer) and extensions.gen_random_bytes(integer); a support hook can rewrite the generator call at plan time. Stop rather than adapt.';
  END IF;

  -- ROUND 8: THE RESOLUTION CANDIDATE SET IS CLOSED, IN BOTH SCHEMAS. The wrapper's string body
  -- re-resolves `extensions.gen_random_bytes(len)` at call time, so a competing overload —
  -- `(integer, boolean DEFAULT false)`, a VARIADIC form, anything callable with one integer —
  -- can make that call ambiguous in a fresh session even though both pinned routines are
  -- byte-identical: installation would pass and every later claim_token default would fail (or
  -- worse, resolve elsewhere). The candidate inventory for the generator NAME must therefore
  -- be exactly the one reviewed one-argument routine in each schema; a second overload of any
  -- shape refuses before any D7 mutation.
  -- ROUND 9: each candidate closes on RESULT SHAPE and ROUTINE KIND too, not input identity
  -- alone. Function identity is (name, input args), so a DROP+CREATE can keep the identity while
  -- swapping the routine for a SETOF/OUT-shaped or procedural twin; an inventory recording input
  -- identity only would accept it. Each element serializes identity args, the rendered result
  -- and prokind, so a result-shape or kind change refuses like a new overload.
  FOREACH v_obj IN ARRAY ARRAY['public','extensions'] LOOP
    SELECT coalesce(array_agg(pg_get_function_identity_arguments(p.oid)
                              || E'\x1f' || coalesce(pg_get_function_result(p.oid), '<none>')
                              || E'\x1f' || p.prokind::text
                              ORDER BY pg_get_function_identity_arguments(p.oid)), '{}')
      INTO v_generator_candidates
      FROM pg_proc p
     WHERE p.pronamespace = (SELECT oid FROM pg_namespace WHERE nspname = v_obj)
       AND p.proname = 'gen_random_bytes';
    IF (v_obj = 'public'     AND v_generator_candidates IS DISTINCT FROM
          ARRAY['len integer' || E'\x1f' || 'bytea' || E'\x1f' || 'f'])
    OR (v_obj = 'extensions' AND v_generator_candidates IS DISTINCT FROM
          ARRAY['integer' || E'\x1f' || 'bytea' || E'\x1f' || 'f']) THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: schema % holds gen_random_bytes candidate set {%}, not exactly the reviewed one-argument bytea function', v_obj,
        array_to_string(v_generator_candidates, '; ')
        USING HINT = 'A second overload (defaulted, variadic or otherwise) can capture or break the wrapper''s one-integer call at parse time, and a DROP+CREATE can change the result shape or routine kind under the same input identity. The candidate inventory is closed over inputs, result shape and kind; stop rather than adapt.';
    END IF;
  END LOOP;

  -- ROUND 9 (AB-D3): the chain links must be PLAIN FUNCTIONS, asserted BEFORE the digest below
  -- ever calls pg_get_functiondef — that renderer raises a raw, illegible ERROR for aggregate
  -- and window routines, so a non-'f' routine wearing a chain name must refuse legibly here
  -- rather than abort inside the serializer.
  IF EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM pg_proc p
     WHERE p.oid IN (to_regprocedure('public.gen_random_bytes(integer)'),
                     to_regprocedure('extensions.gen_random_bytes(integer)'))
       AND p.prokind <> 'f'
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: a claim-token generator routine is not a plain function (prokind <> ''f'') — the reviewed chain is two ordinary functions'
      USING HINT = 'An aggregate, window or procedural routine wearing a chain name is not the reviewed generator. Stop rather than adapt.';
  END IF;

  -- ROUND 8: THE `extensions` NAMESPACE ITSELF IS AN AUTHORITY SURFACE. Function EXECUTE alone
  -- does not make the chain exercisable — the caller also needs USAGE on the schema — and
  -- CREATE on the schema is exactly what an overload injection needs. So the namespace is
  -- pinned as one digest over: exact owner, the RAW nspacl (built-in default rendered when
  -- NULL), and the EFFECTIVE USAGE and CREATE answers (membership paths included, which is
  -- what has_schema_privilege evaluates) for the exact reviewed role classes. A missing schema
  -- serializes as '<absent>' and refuses.
  SELECT encode(pg_catalog.sha256(pg_catalog.convert_to(coalesce(
           (SELECT n.nspowner::regrole::text
                   || E'\x1f' || coalesce((SELECT string_agg(
                          CASE WHEN a.grantee = 0 THEN 'PUBLIC' ELSE a.grantee::regrole::text END
                          || '=' || a.privilege_type || '/' || a.is_grantable::text,
                          E'\x1d' ORDER BY CASE WHEN a.grantee = 0 THEN 'PUBLIC'
                                                ELSE a.grantee::regrole::text END, a.privilege_type)
                          FROM aclexplode(coalesce(n.nspacl, acldefault('n', n.nspowner))) a), '<none>')
                   || E'\x1f' || (SELECT string_agg(
                          r2.rolname
                          || '=USAGE:'  || has_schema_privilege(r2.rolname, n.oid, 'USAGE')::text
                          || ',CREATE:' || has_schema_privilege(r2.rolname, n.oid, 'CREATE')::text,
                          E'\x1d' ORDER BY r2.rolname)
                          FROM (VALUES ('postgres'),('anon'),('authenticated'),('service_role')) AS r2(rolname))
              FROM pg_namespace n WHERE n.nspname = 'extensions'),
           '<absent>'), 'UTF8')), 'hex')
    INTO v_extensions_ns_digest;
  IF v_extensions_ns_digest IS DISTINCT FROM 'c3be8d7d50cf2fa1dffec62dc0d2d369e8c67492b951ac7ca2219672e346b124' THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: the extensions-namespace authority digest is %, not the pinned predecessor baseline', v_extensions_ns_digest
      USING HINT = 'The schema owner, raw ACL, or an effective USAGE/CREATE answer for postgres/anon/authenticated/service_role drifted. USAGE is what makes the claim-token chain exercisable and CREATE is what an overload injection needs; stop rather than adapt.';
  END IF;

  -- And the CREATE negative space, as its own legible arm: no runtime role may be able to
  -- create — and therefore inject a callable overload into — the generator schema.
  IF has_schema_privilege('anon', 'extensions', 'CREATE')
     OR has_schema_privilege('authenticated', 'extensions', 'CREATE')
     OR has_schema_privilege('service_role', 'extensions', 'CREATE') THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: a runtime role holds CREATE on schema extensions — an injected overload could capture the claim-token generator call';
  END IF;

  SELECT encode(pg_catalog.sha256(pg_catalog.convert_to(
           string_agg(coalesce(r.record, '<absent>'), E'\x1e' ORDER BY chain.ord), 'UTF8')), 'hex')
    INTO v_generator_chain_digest
    FROM (VALUES
           (1, 'public.gen_random_bytes(integer)'),
           (2, 'extensions.gen_random_bytes(integer)')
         ) AS chain(ord, ident)
    LEFT JOIN LATERAL (
      SELECT pn.nspname || '.' || p.proname
             || '(' || pg_get_function_identity_arguments(p.oid) || ')'
             || E'\x1f' || p.proowner::regrole::text
             || E'\x1f' || p.prokind::text
             || E'\x1f' || pg_get_functiondef(p.oid)
             || E'\x1f' || coalesce(p.protrftypes::text, '<none>')
             || E'\x1f' || p.prosupport::regproc::text
             || E'\x1f' || coalesce((SELECT e.extname FROM pg_depend dp
                                       JOIN pg_extension e ON e.oid = dp.refobjid
                                      WHERE dp.classid = 'pg_proc'::regclass
                                        AND dp.objid = p.oid
                                        AND dp.refclassid = 'pg_extension'::regclass
                                        AND dp.deptype = 'e'), '<none>')
             -- ROUND 10 — THE COMPLETE FOUR-FIELD RAW ACL RECORD. `aclexplode` yields grantor,
             -- grantee, privilege_type and is_grantable; round 9 serialized only the latter
             -- three, so two ACLs differing solely in WHO granted an otherwise identical
             -- privilege produced the same record and the same digest, and the effective-EXECUTE
             -- vector below cannot close that collision because it is unchanged by a regrant
             -- through a delegate (review round 9, P2). BOTH role OIDs are canonicalized the same
             -- way — role name when nonzero, the literal PUBLIC when zero — and the direction is
             -- spelled unambiguously as grantor->grantee, so no pair of distinct ACL rows can
             -- concatenate into one another's text. The deterministic ordering carries the same
             -- complete tuple in the same order, which also keeps the two rows `aclexplode`
             -- emits for a privilege held WITH GRANT OPTION in a fixed sequence.
             || E'\x1f' || coalesce((SELECT string_agg(
                    CASE WHEN a.grantor = 0 THEN 'PUBLIC' ELSE a.grantor::regrole::text END
                    || '->' || CASE WHEN a.grantee = 0 THEN 'PUBLIC' ELSE a.grantee::regrole::text END
                    || '=' || a.privilege_type || '/' || a.is_grantable::text,
                    E'\x1d' ORDER BY CASE WHEN a.grantor = 0 THEN 'PUBLIC'
                                          ELSE a.grantor::regrole::text END,
                                     CASE WHEN a.grantee = 0 THEN 'PUBLIC'
                                          ELSE a.grantee::regrole::text END,
                                     a.privilege_type, a.is_grantable)
                    FROM aclexplode(coalesce(p.proacl, acldefault('f', p.proowner))) a), '<none>')
             || E'\x1f' || (SELECT string_agg(
                    r2.rolname || '=' || has_function_privilege(r2.rolname, p.oid, 'EXECUTE')::text,
                    E'\x1d' ORDER BY r2.rolname)
                    FROM (VALUES ('postgres'),('anon'),('authenticated'),('service_role')) AS r2(rolname))
             AS record
        FROM pg_proc p
        JOIN pg_namespace pn ON pn.oid = p.pronamespace
       WHERE p.oid = to_regprocedure(chain.ident)
    ) r ON true;
  IF v_generator_chain_digest IS DISTINCT FROM '65fb71a0816673ab2459d3eae7f7229196a26d73e24867e704be1e285c58a5b5' THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: the claim-token generator execution-chain digest is %, not the pinned predecessor baseline', v_generator_chain_digest
      USING HINT = 'The chain public.gen_random_bytes(integer) -> extensions.gen_random_bytes(integer) drifted: a routine is missing, its complete catalog definition (identity, kind, result shape, argument modes/names/defaults, language, volatility, strictness, leakproofness, parallel safety, definer mode, configuration, cost/rows, body or C binding), a transform or planner-support binding, extension membership, the complete raw ACL (every aclexplode row''s grantor, grantee, privilege and grantability), or effective EXECUTE authority moved for postgres, anon, authenticated or service_role. Newly minted claim tokens execute this exact chain; stop rather than adapt.';
  END IF;

  -- 2d. THE COMPLETE POLICY INVENTORY, pinned two ways: per-relation counts (a legible first
  --     diagnostic) and one SHA-256 over the full catalog-normalized rows — name, command,
  --     permissiveness, role list, USING and WITH CHECK expressions as pg_get_expr renders them —
  --     so a predicate edit, an audience change or a permissive/restrictive flip refuses even
  --     when the counts still agree. Unit separators keep adjacent fields from concatenating
  --     into a colliding string.
  IF EXISTS (
    SELECT 1
      FROM (VALUES
        ('academy_profiles',     6), ('availability_slots',   12), ('bookings',             15),
        ('cycles',               13), ('guest_players',        10), ('locations',            6),
        ('profiles',             12), ('slot_priority_claims', 2), ('trainer_profiles',     11)
      ) AS expected(relname, n)
      JOIN pg_class c ON c.oid = to_regclass('public.' || expected.relname)
     WHERE (SELECT count(*) FROM pg_policy p WHERE p.polrelid = c.oid) <> expected.n
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: a Domain-P relation''s policy count drifted from the pinned predecessor baseline (expected, in alphabetical relation order, 6/12/15/13/10/6/12/2/11)';
  END IF;
  -- ROLE NORMALIZATION IS PER-ELEMENT, NEVER A JOIN-AND-HOPE. `polroles` stores PUBLIC as role
  -- OID 0, which has no pg_roles row: a serialization that aggregates only the JOINED names
  -- silently drops PUBLIC whenever a named role is also present, so `TO authenticated` and
  -- `TO authenticated, PUBLIC` would digest identically. Every array element is rendered below,
  -- with OID 0 spelled explicitly, which is what closes that.
  --
  -- AND THE EARLIER VERSION OF THIS COMMENT OVERSTATED THE REACH OF THAT HAZARD, SO IT IS STATED
  -- AS MEASURED. `TO authenticated, PUBLIC` is not a state DDL can leave behind: measured on this
  -- server version, BOTH `CREATE POLICY … TO x, PUBLIC` and `ALTER POLICY … TO x, PUBLIC` store
  -- plain `{0}` and emit the warning `ignoring specified roles other than PUBLIC`. So the
  -- mixed-array reading defect is unreachable through supported DDL, and the per-element rendering
  -- is what keeps the digest honest about the states DIRECT CATALOG WRITES can still produce.
  --
  -- TWO SUCH STATES DEFEAT PER-ELEMENT RENDERING TOO, AND THEY ARE REFUSED HERE RATHER THAN
  -- DIGESTED. The rendering maps every element to a NAME, so any two elements sharing a name are
  -- indistinguishable inside it:
  --   * AN OID WITH NO `pg_roles` ROW renders as the literal `PUBLIC`, colliding with a genuine
  --     OID-0 element — a dangling audience reads as "admits everybody", which is the widest
  --     possible reading, produced silently. `DROP ROLE` cannot create this (a dependent policy
  --     makes it `2BP01`), so it is a direct-catalog-write state; that is exactly the class this
  --     gate's other direct-write arms already refuse.
  --   * A ROLE LITERALLY NAMED `PUBLIC` renders as the string `PUBLIC` as well. That is not
  --     hypothetical: measured, lowercase `public` is reserved (`42939`) but `CREATE ROLE "PUBLIC"`
  --     SUCCEEDS, and the resulting audience is byte-identical to OID 0 under this rendering.
  -- Both are refused BEFORE the digest is computed, so the refusal names the relation, the policy
  -- and the OID instead of surfacing as an unexplained digest mismatch.
  --
  -- AND THE GUARD ALONE DID NOT MAKE THE RENDERING INJECTIVE, WHICH IS WHY THE RENDERING CHANGED.
  -- The predecessor joined the element names with a bare comma, so an audience of the two roles
  -- `"a"` and `"b"` rendered as `a,b` — byte-identical to a SINGLE role legally named `"a,b"`.
  -- Neither state is defective, so the guard above admits both, and the digest could not tell a
  -- two-role audience from a one-role audience whose name happens to contain the separator. Every
  -- element is now LENGTH-PREFIXED (`<characters>:<name>`) before the join, which makes the
  -- rendering uniquely decodable and therefore injective over every legal role name: the reader
  -- takes the count, consumes exactly that many characters, and cannot be misled by any character
  -- the name itself contains. The pinned digest below was recomputed ONCE under this rendering
  -- against the same predecessor lineage; it still describes the healthy predecessor and nothing
  -- else.
  --
  -- THE RESIDUAL IS DISCLOSED RATHER THAN OVERSTATED. What is now injective is the AUDIENCE field.
  -- The record framing around it still separates fields with `\x1f` and records with `\x1e`, and
  -- `relname` is safe there because it is bounded to the nine literal names above, while `polname`
  -- is not bounded at all — a policy deliberately named with one of those two control characters
  -- could still frame-shift the record. That is a different defect from the one fixed here, it is
  -- not what this rendering claims to close, and it is recorded rather than papered over.
  SELECT string_agg(t.line, '; ' ORDER BY t.line) INTO v_audience_defect
    FROM (SELECT c.relname || '.' || p.polname || ' -> role oid '
                 || e.roleoid::pg_catalog.text
                 || CASE WHEN r.rolname IS NULL THEN ' (no pg_roles row)'
                         ELSE ' (a role literally named PUBLIC)' END AS line
            FROM pg_catalog.pg_policy p
            JOIN pg_catalog.pg_class c ON c.oid = p.polrelid
            JOIN pg_catalog.pg_namespace n ON n.oid = c.relnamespace
            CROSS JOIN LATERAL pg_catalog.unnest(p.polroles) AS e(roleoid)
            LEFT JOIN pg_catalog.pg_roles r ON r.oid = e.roleoid
           WHERE n.nspname = 'public'
             AND c.relname = ANY (v_tables)
             AND e.roleoid <> 0
             AND (r.rolname IS NULL OR r.rolname = 'PUBLIC')) t;
  IF v_audience_defect IS NOT NULL THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: a Domain-P policy audience cannot be rendered injectively (%) — refusing to digest an audience that reads as PUBLIC without being PUBLIC', v_audience_defect
      USING HINT = 'A polroles element that resolves to no role, or to a role literally named PUBLIC, renders identically to role OID 0 — the widest audience there is. The two states arrive by different routes: a dangling OID is not reachable through supported DDL at all (DROP ROLE on a policy audience raises 2BP01), so it is a direct catalog write; a role literally named PUBLIC is reachable through ORDINARY DDL, because CREATE ROLE "PUBLIC" succeeds where lowercase public is reserved. Inspect it; stop rather than adapt.';
  END IF;
  SELECT encode(pg_catalog.sha256(pg_catalog.convert_to(coalesce(string_agg(
           c.relname || E'\x1f' || p.polname || E'\x1f' || p.polcmd::text || E'\x1f'
           || p.polpermissive::text || E'\x1f'
           || coalesce((SELECT string_agg(
                          pg_catalog.length(a.nm)::pg_catalog.text || ':' || a.nm, ','
                          ORDER BY a.nm)
                          FROM unnest(p.polroles) AS e(roleoid)
                          LEFT JOIN pg_roles r ON r.oid = e.roleoid
                          CROSS JOIN LATERAL (SELECT CASE WHEN r.rolname IS NULL THEN 'PUBLIC'
                                                          ELSE r.rolname::pg_catalog.text END)
                                     AS a(nm)), '<none>') || E'\x1f'
           || coalesce(pg_get_expr(p.polqual, p.polrelid), '<none>') || E'\x1f'
           || coalesce(pg_get_expr(p.polwithcheck, p.polrelid), '<none>'),
           E'\x1e' ORDER BY c.relname, p.polname), ''), 'UTF8')), 'hex')
    INTO v_policy_digest
    FROM pg_policy p
    JOIN pg_class c ON c.oid = p.polrelid
    JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = c.relnamespace
   WHERE n.nspname = 'public' AND c.relname = ANY (v_tables);
  IF v_policy_digest IS DISTINCT FROM '02e09ecf706baf90456478120273604865960502f70050106b6343a72a9bc45d' THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ABC-27 Stage-0: the Domain-P policy inventory digest is %, not the pinned predecessor baseline', v_policy_digest
      USING HINT = 'A policy predicate, audience, command or permissiveness changed on one of the nine relations. This is not the reviewed predecessor; stop rather than adapt.';
  END IF;

  -- ── AND THIS COPY OF THE AUDIENCE GUARD AND THE POLICY DIGEST IS NOT THE AUTHORITATIVE ONE ──
  --
  -- Same standing as §0a's baseline, for the same measured reason: this block holds no lock on any
  -- of the nine relations when it ends, so a policy committed on any of them after this point is
  -- invisible here and changes nothing about what this block already concluded. The guard and the
  -- digest are re-run — in this exact order, over this exact rendering — inside the final
  -- re-verification, under the canonical `SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE` set and in the same statement as the
  -- policy DDL they protect. That copy is the authority. This one is a fast-fail diagnostic that
  -- stops a drifted database at the top of the file instead of halfway down it.
  --
  -- The remaining Stage-0 inventories below (triggers, columns, constraints, indexes, the wrapper
  -- and generator chains) are NOT re-run under the locks, and they do not claim to be: they pin
  -- shapes this unit does not itself narrow, and the installation window — not a catalog read — is
  -- what the approved model uses to bound concurrent change to them.

  -- 2e. THE DML TRIGGER INVENTORY, exact and bidirectional — pinned as each trigger's ENABLED
  --     state plus its COMPLETE catalog-rendered definition (timing, events, column lists, level,
  --     WHEN clause, arguments, function identity), not a name/mask tuple (Stage 7.4-A review: a
  --     disabled `trg_guard_guest_bridge_columns`, or the same tuple recreated `WHEN (false)`,
  --     matched the old comparison while the reviewed guard never fired). What fires during a
  --     Domain-P write is part of what the P writers were reviewed against, so the pinned set is
  --     the COMPLETE production surface of the nine — all 44 user triggers the repository lineage
  --     installs and never drops — not a hand-picked subset of the ones this unit finds
  --     interesting. Stage 7.4-C: the earlier pin was 18 rows taken from a synthetic predecessor
  --     whose nine relations were fixture stubs; on the real chain `bookings` alone carries SEVEN
  --     (the tier/capacity gate, the auto-follow, both payment-column guards, the lifecycle
  --     recorder, the person stamp and the audit stamp), and a pin that did not know about six of
  --     them could not have refused their removal. Every row below is the catalog's own
  --     `pg_get_triggerdef` output on a clean install of the full repaired lineage plus
  --     `20261118115500`. The triggers THIS migration installs are created AFTER this gate runs
  --     and are pinned separately by §10c against the installed catalog.
  --
  --     BOTH DIRECTIONS SHARE THE ONE LITERAL SET (review-2 P1): the earlier shape wrote the
  --     expected rows out twice and the second copy silently lagged the first, so a MISSING
  --     member of the newer rows did not refuse. One CTE now feeds a symmetric difference — a
  --     missing, extra, disabled, renamed or redefined trigger refuses identically, and the two
  --     arms cannot drift apart because there is nothing left to keep in sync.
  IF EXISTS (
    WITH expected(relname, tgname, tgenabled, tgdef) AS (VALUES
       ('academy_profiles', 'academy_profiles_set_slug', 'O',
        'CREATE TRIGGER academy_profiles_set_slug BEFORE INSERT ON public.academy_profiles FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION set_academy_slug_on_create()'),
       ('academy_profiles', 'on_academy_profile_created', 'O',
        'CREATE TRIGGER on_academy_profile_created AFTER INSERT ON public.academy_profiles FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION trigger_academy_onboarding_emails()'),
       ('academy_profiles', 'on_academy_subscription_activated', 'O',
        'CREATE TRIGGER on_academy_subscription_activated AFTER UPDATE ON public.academy_profiles FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION trigger_academy_paid_plan_emails()'),
       ('academy_profiles', 'set_academy_trial_trigger', 'O',
        'CREATE TRIGGER set_academy_trial_trigger BEFORE INSERT ON public.academy_profiles FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION set_academy_trial()'),
       ('academy_profiles', 'trg_academy_owner_programs_guard', 'O',
        'CREATE TRIGGER trg_academy_owner_programs_guard BEFORE DELETE ON public.academy_profiles FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION guard_owner_has_no_programs(''academy'')'),
       ('academy_profiles', 'update_academy_profiles_updated_at', 'O',
        'CREATE TRIGGER update_academy_profiles_updated_at BEFORE UPDATE ON public.academy_profiles FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION update_updated_at_column()'),
       ('availability_slots', 'trg_inherit_cycle_split_payment', 'O',
        'CREATE TRIGGER trg_inherit_cycle_split_payment BEFORE INSERT OR UPDATE OF cyclus_id ON public.availability_slots FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION inherit_cycle_split_payment_on_slot()'),
       ('availability_slots', 'trg_trainer_slot_overlap_ins', 'O',
        'CREATE TRIGGER trg_trainer_slot_overlap_ins AFTER INSERT ON public.availability_slots FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION check_trainer_slot_overlap()'),
       ('availability_slots', 'trg_trainer_slot_overlap_upd', 'O',
        'CREATE TRIGGER trg_trainer_slot_overlap_upd AFTER UPDATE OF trainer_id, start_time, end_time ON public.availability_slots FOR EACH ROW WHEN (((old.trainer_id IS DISTINCT FROM new.trainer_id) OR (old.start_time IS DISTINCT FROM new.start_time) OR (old.end_time IS DISTINCT FROM new.end_time))) EXECUTE FUNCTION check_trainer_slot_overlap()'),
       ('availability_slots', 'validate_public_release_status_trg', 'O',
        'CREATE TRIGGER validate_public_release_status_trg BEFORE INSERT OR UPDATE OF public_release_status ON public.availability_slots FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION validate_public_release_status()'),
       ('bookings', 'trg_auto_follow_trainer_on_booking', 'O',
        'CREATE TRIGGER trg_auto_follow_trainer_on_booking AFTER INSERT ON public.bookings FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION auto_follow_trainer_on_booking()'),
       ('bookings', 'trg_enforce_booking_slot_tier', 'O',
        'CREATE TRIGGER trg_enforce_booking_slot_tier BEFORE INSERT OR UPDATE ON public.bookings FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION enforce_booking_slot_tier()'),
       ('bookings', 'trg_protect_booking_financial_columns_for_players', 'O',
        'CREATE TRIGGER trg_protect_booking_financial_columns_for_players BEFORE UPDATE ON public.bookings FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION protect_booking_financial_columns_for_players()'),
       ('bookings', 'trg_protect_booking_payment_on_player_insert', 'O',
        'CREATE TRIGGER trg_protect_booking_payment_on_player_insert BEFORE INSERT ON public.bookings FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION protect_booking_payment_on_player_insert()'),
       ('bookings', 'trg_record_booking_lifecycle_event', 'O',
